window.STONEFOLD_GUIDE=[{"p":"/guide/#start-keeping","t":"Choosing what to keep","g":"Getting started","x":"How do I turn a part on or off? Open Settings. Go to Sections. Switch a part on or off: Calendar, Reminders, Notes, Budget, Tools or Files. Welcome keeps every part the first time you open the app: the Calendar, Reminders, Notes, Budget, Tools and Files. A part that is off is hidden. Nothing you put in it is touched, and it is all there when you turn it on again. Nothing is made until you go in from the first launch’s page: then every part kept but the Budget is started, its encrypted file made, and the Budget waits for its own setup. A part you turn on later starts the first time you use it. Extras Each part has switches of its own, on its own page under Settings ▸ Sections once the part is started. When a part is first started, every one of them is on but Show my other calendars, which reads your own calendars on this device and asks first. Turn off any you don’t want. A part you used before keeps its switches as you left them. The first time you open it After Welcome, one page offers the look: each theme, font, way of setting figures and density drawn as it looks, with text size on the Mac, and a sample that changes as you choose. Start goes into the app. Skip goes in with the look as it was. Everything on the page is in Settings ▸ Look too, and Set Up Again brings the page back: on the Mac in the Help menu, on the iPhone in Settings under Help and About. Going in from it again starts nothing. Nothing asks for permission when the app first opens. The system asks to allow alerts the first time something wants one: an event given an alert, a reminder given a time, an alarm or a timer set, or a document given an expiry day."},{"p":"/guide/#start-parts-together","t":"How the parts work together","g":"Getting started","x":"Each part of Stonefold keeps its own encrypted file, and two switches in Settings let the parts share what they know. Both are on from the start, and you can turn either off. Bills on the calendar Each bill shows on its due day, with its amount. The switch is Bills on the Calendar and in Reminders, in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Budget. Bills in Reminders With the same switch on, waiting bills sit at the top of Today. Choose Paid and it is recorded in the budget. See Bills and paydays ask first . Reminders on the calendar With Reminders on the Calendar on, in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Calendar, a reminder with a day shows under that day’s events, until it is done. See More for the calendar ."},{"p":"/guide/#start-look","t":"How the app looks","g":"Getting started","x":"Where do I change it? In Settings ▸ Look. It changes how the app looks, and nothing in it. Theme Editor, which the app starts in, is slate, as a code editor is, with soft light words, a clear blue, and code and charts in several colors. Sage is a deep green with sage, its headings in a book face. Linen is its light page, with deep green. Night is near black with a clear blue. Ember is a warm near black with ochre. Paper is light, the color of paper, with deep teal. Dusk is a deep violet with lavender. Clay is warm plaster with terracotta. Plain is the system’s own colors, light or dark as the device is set. Choosing a theme also sets the font, the figures and the density to the theme’s own. Each can then be changed by itself. Font and figures Font is one of the faces that come with the device: System, Rounded, Serif or Monospace. Figures sets amounts, dates and times in a monospace face, or in the text’s own face with digits of one width. Either way, columns of amounts line up. Code Cursor Code Cursor sets the insertion point in a Code note: a Line, as everywhere else, or a Block one character wide, as a terminal draws it. On the Mac Density sets how tightly rows and the room between things are set. Compact puts more in sight. Roomy gives more room. Text Size makes the words of the whole window smaller or larger. The menus stay as the Mac draws them. A note keeps its own size, set from Notes ▸ Bigger Text. Tabs puts the window’s tabs along the top, or down the left Side. See Tabs and desks . App Icon is Standard, Match My Theme, which follows the theme, or one theme’s own. On the Mac it starts on Match My Theme. The Dock shows it while the app is open. On the iPhone Density sets the room between groups. Compact never makes a row shorter, so every row is still easy to touch. The size of the words is the one you choose in the iPhone’s own Settings. App Icon is Standard, Match My Theme, or one theme’s own. It is the app’s icon on the Home Screen, and the iPhone tells you each time it changes. These choices are this device’s own settings. They are not in an export, or in the app’s own backups."},{"p":"/guide/#start-settings-by-group","t":"Settings, page by page","g":"Getting started","x":"Settings opens on a short first page of rows, each opening a page of its own, on the iPhone and the Mac. On the Mac the same pages run down the side. A page shows only what has something to show: a part’s switches and alerts once the part is started, a file’s backups once the file is there. Look. Theme, font, figures, Code Cursor and density, and on the Mac the text size and the tabs. The app icon, on the Mac Change Keys, and the order of the sections, with on the Mac Today’s parts and on the iPhone which sections are on the tab bar. Sections. Which parts you keep, and the calculator’s switches. Each part you keep has a page of its own switches under it. The Budget’s holds the switch for bills on the calendar and in Reminders. The Lock. Alerts, while a part you keep sets them: the reminders’, the calendar’s and the budget’s bill reminders. Notes set no alert. Backups and Export. Backups and saved copies, by file, Export Everything and each part’s own export, and Bring Mine Over for the Calendar and the Reminders. Privacy. What Leaves This Device, and Where Your Things Are Kept. Help and About. Help, Budgeting Words while the Budget is kept, and Acknowledgements. Under each switch, one line says what it does. The switch for iCloud sync is in Settings too. The command box opens the page that holds a setting. On the Mac the same pages are in the window as well as in the Settings window. See Everything in the window ."},{"p":"/guide/#start-adding-from-outside","t":"Adding from the Home Screen and Shortcuts","g":"Getting started","x":"On the iPhone Touch and hold the app’s icon on the Home Screen. Choose a button. There is one for each part you keep: an event, a reminder, a note and a purchase. The app opens where you add that thing. The Shortcuts app has one action for this app. Make a shortcut with it and choose what it starts, from the parts you keep. A shortcut can be run from Control Center, from the Action button on an iPhone that has one, or by tapping the back of the iPhone. On the Mac The Shortcuts app has one action for this app. Make a shortcut with it and choose what it starts: an event, a reminder, a note or a purchase, for the parts you keep. Running the shortcut opens the app where you add that thing. Good to know Neither takes anything you type. What you add is typed in the app. If the app is locked, it asks to unlock first, and what you asked for opens once it is unlocked. A part that is hidden has no button and is not offered to a shortcut. A part you haven’t started is only shown."},{"p":"/guide/#start-the-lock","t":"The lock","g":"Getting started","x":"How does it work? With Lock the app on, in Settings ▸ The Lock, opening the app asks once: Face ID or Touch ID, or the device’s passcode or password if that fails. It starts on once you keep the Notes, the Budget or Files. By default, anyone who can unlock your device can open the app, unless you lock the budget or the notes with a passphrase. When does it lock? On the Mac, when the Mac sleeps, its screen locks, another user takes over or the window is closed, and after the time without use chosen in Settings ▸ The Lock. Lock Now is ⌃⌘L . On the iPhone, after you leave it, and after a time without use, each as chosen in Settings ▸ The Lock, and at once when the iPhone itself locks. Locked, the app shows none of your words or numbers. Until you first use a part there is nothing to lock, so it doesn’t ask."},{"p":"/guide/#start-a-passphrase","t":"A passphrase on the budget or the notes","g":"Getting started","x":"How do I add one? Neither has a passphrase unless you add one. In Settings ▸ The Lock, “Lock the budget with a passphrase” and “Lock the notes with a passphrase” each add one. With both on there is one passphrase, typed once. If the device has Face ID or Touch ID, that still opens them day to day. When it isn’t used, the app asks for the passphrase. If I forget it Nobody can get a forgotten passphrase back: not us, and not Apple. It isn’t stored anywhere. While Face ID or Touch ID still opens them, Settings can turn the passphrase off and you can set a new one. Otherwise your recovery sheet, if you made one, opens what it covers. With no sheet, nothing does. What can’t have one The calendar and the reminders can’t have a passphrase. The app opens them itself, so that your alerts can be set. Files has no passphrase of its own either: it opens with the one unlock, with the notes and the budget."},{"p":"/guide/#start-recovery-sheet","t":"The recovery sheet","g":"Getting started","x":"What is it? One page, with a code for each thing it covers. It is the way back if the device’s keys are lost, or a passphrase is forgotten. How do I make one? The app offers it once when you first go in, for everything made then, and again when a file is made later, such as the budget’s. Or open Settings ▸ The Lock and choose Make the Recovery Sheet…, which covers whatever is open then. Once you have one, the button reads Make a New Recovery Sheet… instead. Keeping it Print it and keep it where you would keep a key. The app never stores a code. A new sheet stops the old one working, for everything the new one covers."},{"p":"/guide/#start-backups","t":"Backups, and saving a copy","g":"Getting started","x":"Automatic backups Once a day, when the app is unlocked, what you keep is backed up if something changed: the calendar and reminders together, the notes, and the budget, each encrypted with its own key. The newest 30 daily backups of each are kept, and 10 made with Back Up Now. Files keeps a file of its own, backed up the same way. A backup of Files holds the list of your documents, their details, the words found in them and what opens them, not a second copy of each document. Files has no Save a Copy of its own as a whole: Save a Copy… on a document writes that document out as it is. Putting a backup back Settings ▸ Backups and Export puts one back. How it was is saved first, so putting one back can be undone the same way. Save a Copy Save a Copy… writes an encrypted copy of one file wherever you choose, sealed with a passphrase you type for that copy. A budget or notes locked with a passphrase keep that passphrase on their copies. Settings ▸ Privacy ▸ What Leaves This Device says what a backup of the device itself holds."},{"p":"/guide/#start-a-new-device","t":"A new Mac or a new iPhone","g":"Getting started","x":"What the new device has On a Mac, the keys to what you keep are in that Mac’s login keychain. On an iPhone, they are kept for that iPhone alone. A new iPhone set up from the old one’s backup has what you kept and not its keys. There, your recovery sheet opens it if you made one, and a budget or notes locked with a passphrase open with the passphrase. Moving with a saved copy On the old device, use Save a Copy… for each of the calendar and reminders, the notes and the budget. On the new device, use Open a Saved Copy…, on the Welcome screen or in Settings ▸ Backups and Export. A copy is separate. What you add on one device doesn’t appear on the other. Moving with iCloud sync If you use iCloud sync, a new device gets the key from one of your devices, with a code you scan or type. Your recovery sheet opens what it covers too. See iCloud sync ."},{"p":"/guide/#start-leaving-with-your-things","t":"Leaving with your things","g":"Getting started","x":"Your events, reminders, notes and budget can be exported in formats other apps can open. How do I do it? Open Settings ▸ Backups and Export. Choose Export Everything…. It says first what goes in and what is left out: a part that is hidden, not started, or waiting for its passphrase. Choose a folder. Stonefold writes one new folder inside it. What is in the folder The calendar is one .ics file of every event, with its repeats and its alert, which calendar apps can open. The reminders are one CSV file, a row for each reminder in your lists, which spreadsheet apps can open. The notes are a folder with a plain text file for each note, which any text editor opens. A deck’s cards are written as a file of their own, the kind Anki brings in, without pictures or your answers. The budget is one .xlsx workbook, which spreadsheet apps can open: your transactions, accounts, categories and bills, with a summary of each month. Files is a folder holding every document as it is, in the same folders. On the Mac, with Saved Views on, the notes’ saved views go in a CSV file in the notes’ folder, and the reminders’ in a CSV file of their own. The pictures you put in panels go in a folder named Pictures, each a JPEG file, named for when you added it. What is not in it The alarms, timers and cities of Tools aren’t in the export, and the sheet says so. They are kept with the calendar and reminders, so Save a Copy… of the calendar and reminders carries them. Recently Deleted is left out. Some marks and settings stay behind: a calendar’s color, which notes are pinned, and in the budget its targets and paydays. Keep in mind Nothing in that folder is encrypted. Anyone who can open it can read it, so keep it as you would keep the things themselves. Each part’s own export is in Settings ▸ Backups and Export too. To move to another device, use Save a Copy… instead: a saved copy stays encrypted, and opens there as it was here."},{"p":"/guide/#start-what-stays-private","t":"What stays private, and what leaves","g":"Getting started","x":"No account. No sign-up. No ads, no tracking, no analytics, and no AI. What you keep is stored on your devices, encrypted. It works offline. We can’t see what’s in it. What leaves your device Settings ▸ Privacy ▸ What Leaves This Device says it in plain sentences. With sync off, nothing leaves, unless you save it somewhere yourself. With sync on, sealed changes go to your own iCloud, and Apple can see that your Apple Account is syncing, and when. A saved copy stays encrypted, sealed with its passphrase. An export isn’t encrypted, and the app asks before it makes one. The recovery sheet is a page you print. It opens what it covers, so keep it where you would keep a key. An alert is set on your device and rings from it. Nothing is sent to bring it. A backup of the device itself holds what you keep here, still encrypted. Settings ▸ Privacy ▸ What Leaves This Device says what it holds. Files Your files stay on your device, each sealed by itself with a key of its own, inside the app’s encrypted storage. The words inside a file are found on your device, for search: a PDF’s own text, and with Find Words in Pictures and Scans on, words in pictures, read by Apple’s Vision framework with no language model. Nothing is sent anywhere. A file goes out of the app only when you choose Save a Copy… or Export Everything, and then as it is, not sealed, where you choose. On the iPhone, the camera is used only when you choose Scan Document, and the pages go straight into Files. A reminder before a file expires says only “A document expires soon.”, never the file’s name."},{"p":"/guide/#start-icloud-sync","t":"iCloud sync","g":"Getting started","x":"What it is Sync is a switch in Settings, and it starts off. When you turn it on, each change is sealed on your device first, with a key only your devices hold. It then travels through your own iCloud to your other devices. Sync is end-to-end encrypted. Only your devices hold the key. It runs through your own iCloud, and we run no servers. What it covers Every section you keep, Files included: the Calendar, Reminders, Notes, Budget, Tools and Files. What Apple can see That your Apple Account is syncing, and when. Adding a device A new device gets the key from one of your devices, with a code you scan or type. Your recovery sheet opens what it covers too. The price of this privacy If you lose every device and every copy of the key, nobody can open what you kept, including us. Print the recovery sheet and keep it where you would keep a key. If you don’t want it Leave it off. Every part of Stonefold works without it, and nothing leaves your device."},{"p":"/guide/#calendar-adding-an-event","t":"Adding an event","g":"Calendar","x":"On the Mac Select a day and press ⌘N . Type the event’s title and press Return. The selected day’s events are listed on the right. Double-click one to change it, or select it and press Return. On the iPhone Tap +, on the month or on a day. Type the event’s title and tap Add. From the month the event is on today. From a day, it is on that day. Tap a day to see its events in order, and tap an event to change it. The form A new event is all day until you turn All day off. The form also has Place, Starts, Ends and Notes."},{"p":"/guide/#calendar-moving-around","t":"Moving around the calendar","g":"Calendar","x":"On the Mac The arrow keys move by a day and by a week. ⌘[ and ⌘] go to the month before and after, and ⌘T comes back to today. Today’s number is in a filled circle. A day shows up to three titles, then how many more. The day’s list on the right has them all. On the iPhone Swipe the month to go to the one before or after. Today comes back to today. Today’s number is in a filled circle. A day has a dot for each of up to three events. Tap the day to see them all. At the largest text sizes the month is a list of its days, with every title."},{"p":"/guide/#calendar-the-year","t":"The year at a glance","g":"Calendar","x":"How do I turn it on? In Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Calendar, turn on Year. On the Mac, Year in the sidebar, or ⌥⌘4 , shows the twelve months of the selected day’s year. ⌥⌘[ and ⌥⌘] go to the year before and after, and ⌘T comes back to today. On the iPhone, Year on the month shows the twelve months of its year. The arrows at the top go to the year before and after. Reading it Today’s number is in a filled circle. A day with something on it is shaded, today too: lightly for one thing, more for two or three, most for four or more. Events, reminders shown on the calendar and bills each count. On the Mac, click a day to see its events in the day’s list on the right. Double-click a day, or click a month’s name, to open that month. On the iPhone, tap a month to open it. At the largest text sizes the year is a list of its months, each with how many days have something on."},{"p":"/guide/#calendar-alerts","t":"Alerts for events","g":"Calendar","x":"How do I add an alert? In Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Calendar, turn on Alerts. In an event’s form, choose Alert: at the time of the event, or before it. For an all-day event the alert is at nine in the morning, on the day or before. The first time you choose an alert, the system asks whether the app may alert you, unless it has asked already. What an alert shows An event’s alert has one button, Snooze for 10 Minutes, which brings it back ten minutes later. An alert shows the event’s title, never its place or notes. With “Show the words in alerts” off, in Settings under Alerts, it says just “Event”. An alert is set on your device and rings from it. Nothing is sent to bring it."},{"p":"/guide/#calendar-more-for-the-calendar","t":"More for the calendar","g":"Calendar","x":"Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Calendar turns on more: Repeating, Alerts, More calendars, Week, Year, Show my other calendars and Time zones. When the calendar is first started each is on but Show my other calendars, which asks first. US Holidays is there too, and on. On the Mac, Features at the foot of the sidebar shows the same switches. Repeating events With Repeating on, changing one day of a repeating event asks Change Only This One or Change This and All Later, and deleting it asks Delete Only This One or Delete This and All Later. Reminders and bills on the calendar With the Reminders kept too, Reminders on the Calendar, in the same place, shows a reminder that has a day on that day, under the day’s events, until it is done. With the Budget kept too, a switch in the same place shows its bills on their due days, with their amounts and whether they are paid. Turning a switch off Turning a feature off hides what it adds and changes nothing in your calendar. An event that repeats keeps repeating."},{"p":"/guide/#calendar-tags-and-templates","t":"Tags and templates in the calendar","g":"Calendar","x":"Tags Turn on Tags in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Calendar, then write a word with # in front of it in an event’s title or notes, such as #garden. The sidebar lists every tag with how many events hold it. Choose one to see what is coming that holds it, for a year ahead. Double-click one to see it on its day. Templates Turn on Templates in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Calendar. Any event that holds #template in its title or notes is a template. Choose a day, then Calendar ▸ New Event from Template… ( ⌥⌘N ), and pick one. The new event has the template’s title, place, notes, calendar, times and alert, without the #template, on the day you chose. It doesn’t repeat. The template stays as it is, and Edit ▸ Undo takes the new event back."},{"p":"/guide/#calendar-copying-from-apples-calendar","t":"Copying from Apple’s Calendar","g":"Calendar","x":"How do I do it? Open Settings ▸ Backups and Export and choose Bring Mine Over… for the Calendar. The screen asks first. Choose Continue, and the system asks whether the app may use your calendars. Answer no, and nothing is read or copied. Choose which of Apple’s calendars to copy from, and the day to start from. The screen says how many events will come before anything is copied. Choose Bring In. What comes over Titles, places, notes, days and times come over, and repeating events whose way of repeating this app has. One that repeats another way is left out and named afterwards, so you can add it by hand. Alerts, invitations and attachments stay behind. Afterwards Nothing stays linked. Nothing in Apple’s Calendar is changed, and a change made there afterwards does not show here. Doing it again copies the events again."},{"p":"/guide/#calendar-your-other-calendars","t":"Your other calendars","g":"Calendar","x":"How do I turn it on? In Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Calendar, turn on Show my other calendars. Your calendars in Apple’s Calendar on this device are drawn beside these, in their own colors. The first time you turn it on, the system asks whether the app may use your calendars. Using them On the Mac, they are read only. Double-click one in the day’s list to see it. It can be changed only in Apple’s Calendar. Hide one under On this Mac in the sidebar. On the iPhone, they are read only. Tap one to see it. It can be changed only in Apple’s Calendar. Hide one in Calendars, in the menu on the month. What it means for privacy They stay where they are, protected as Apple protects them. Nothing of theirs is copied into this app’s files, changed or sent, and this app’s encryption is for its own calendar only. If reading them is off Settings says so and has Open Settings. Turning the feature off stops reading them. The permission itself is in System Settings ▸ Privacy & Security ▸ Calendars on the Mac, and in the iPhone’s Settings ▸ Privacy & Security ▸ Calendars."},{"p":"/guide/#calendar-us-holidays","t":"US Holidays","g":"Calendar","x":"US Holidays come with the app: the eleven federal holidays, and Valentine’s Day, Easter Sunday, Mother’s Day, Father’s Day, Halloween, Christmas Eve and New Year’s Eve. They show from the start, all day, in their own color. Observed days When a federal holiday falls on a Saturday, the Friday before shows it again, “observed”: that is the federal day off. When it falls on a Sunday, the Monday after does. What they are They are read only and set no alerts. The app works them out itself, with nothing fetched, and they are in no export, because they aren’t yours. How do I hide them? Turn off US Holidays in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Calendar. With More calendars on, you can also hide them on this device alone, under Calendars in the Mac’s sidebar or in Calendars, in the menu on the month, on the iPhone."},{"p":"/guide/#calendar-bringing-in-a-calendar-file","t":"Bringing in a calendar file","g":"Calendar","x":"How do I do it? Save the calendar file from your browser. Its name ends in .ics. On the Mac, choose Calendar ▸ Bring In a Calendar File…, or the same button in Settings ▸ Backups and Export. On the iPhone, tap Bring In a Calendar File… in Settings ▸ Backups and Export. Choose the file. The screen says how many events will come in before anything is copied. With More calendars on, choose Into: a new calendar named from the file, or one of yours. Choose Bring In. Undo takes it all back. What comes in Titles, places, notes, days and times come in, and repeating events whose way of repeating this app has. One that repeats another way is left out and named afterwards. Alerts and invitations stay behind. A link in the file comes in as words in the notes, and nothing opens it. When the schedule changes Nothing stays linked: the file is read once, when you choose it, and nothing is fetched. Save the new file and bring it into the same calendar. Events brought in before are brought up to date, not added twice: a game moved to another day or time moves. One the file now says is canceled, or one still to come that the newer file no longer holds, goes to Recently Deleted."},{"p":"/guide/#calendar-deleted-events","t":"Deleted events","g":"Calendar","x":"A deleted event waits in Recently Deleted until you empty it. Nothing is removed after a number of days. Choose it there to put it back as it was. Undo On the Mac, Edit ▸ Undo takes back the last change. On the iPhone, Undo, on the month and on a day, takes back the last change. So does shaking the iPhone. Wiping Delete Now wipes one thing, and Empty wipes everything there, days and times too. Each asks first. What is wiped can’t be brought back. Backups made before then still hold it until they age out."},{"p":"/guide/#reminders-adding-a-reminder","t":"Adding a reminder","g":"Reminders","x":"How do I add one? On the Mac, on Today or in a list, press ⌘N , type what to remember in New Reminder, and press Return. On the iPhone, type what to remember in New Reminder, at the bottom of Today or of a list, and press Return. While you type, Day and Time show above it, and give it a day and a time. On Today the day is already today. A time needs a day. A reminder with no day waits in its list and is never in Today. Changing one On the Mac, click a reminder to see it in the inspector, and change its title, notes, day, time or list there. On the iPhone, tap a reminder to change its title, notes, day, time or list."},{"p":"/guide/#reminders-today-and-marking-done","t":"Today, and marking done","g":"Reminders","x":"Today shows what is overdue and what is due today. A reminder due today stays there all day, even after its time has passed. Marking done On the Mac, the circle beside a reminder marks it done. So does ⇧⌘C , for the one selected. Mark Not Done puts it back. On the iPhone, the circle beside a reminder marks it done. Mark Not Done, on its own screen, puts it back. Nothing changes by itself. An overdue reminder waits until you mark it done or give it another day."},{"p":"/guide/#reminders-lists","t":"Lists","g":"Reminders","x":"Every reminder is in a list. You start with one, To Do. Adding and moving On the Mac, New List adds another, and a reminder moves to another list with List, in the inspector. On the iPhone, New List, on Lists, adds another, and a reminder moves to another list with List, on its own screen. Deleting a list Deleting a list sends it to Recently Deleted with the reminders in it. Choosing it there brings them back together. The last list can’t be deleted, because there must always be one to add to."},{"p":"/guide/#reminders-alerts-for-reminders","t":"Alerts for reminders","g":"Reminders","x":"A reminder with a day and a time alerts at that time, on this device’s clock. The first time you give a reminder a time, the system asks whether the app may alert you, unless it has asked already. The buttons An alert has two buttons: Done, and Snooze for 10 Minutes. Done marks the reminder done, even while the app is locked. Snooze brings the alert back ten minutes later. Two choices Settings ▸ Alerts has two choices. With “Show the words in alerts” off, a reminder’s alert says just “Reminder”. With “Alert for reminders with only a day” on, a reminder with a day and no time alerts on its day at the time you choose. How many are set The soonest alerts are set first. Opening the app sets the later ones as their turn comes. An alert is set on your device and rings from it. Nothing is sent to bring it."},{"p":"/guide/#reminders-more-for-reminders","t":"More for reminders","g":"Reminders","x":"Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Reminders turns on more: Repeating, Typed Dates, Flags, Early Alerts, Habits, Maintenance Logs and Boards. Each is on once the reminders are first started. On the Mac, Features at the foot of the sidebar shows them. Repeating A repeating reminder makes its next one when you mark this one done, and not before. Turning a switch off Turning a feature off hides what it adds and changes nothing you entered. A reminder that already repeats keeps repeating."},{"p":"/guide/#reminders-tags-in-reminders","t":"Tags in reminders","g":"Reminders","x":"Tags Turn on Tags in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Reminders, then write a word with # in front of it in a reminder’s title or notes, such as #errands. The sidebar lists every tag with how many reminders still to do hold it. Choose one to see them, from every list. Capitals and accents don’t matter, and nothing is added to a reminder for its tags: they are read from its words. A reminder marked done leaves its tags’ counts. Templates Turn on Templates, and any list whose name holds #template is a template. Reminder ▸ New List from Template… ( ⌥⌘N ) makes a new list with its reminders still to do, with their notes and without their days. The template stays as it is, and one Undo takes the new list back."},{"p":"/guide/#reminders-saved-views","t":"Saved views in your reminders","g":"Reminders","x":"How do I save one? In Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Reminders, turn on Saved Views. Choose a list in the sidebar, or, with Tags on, a tag. Saving a tag, choose Every List, or one list to see only its reminders. Choose Reminder ▸ Save This View… ( ⌥⌘S ). Using your views Your views are in the sidebar, under Saved Views. Choose one to see its list, or the reminders that hold its tag. Control-click a view to rename it, move it up or down, or delete it, or drag it to another place. A deleted view waits in Recently Deleted. Export the Saved Views as CSV…, in Settings ▸ Backups and Export, writes them as a file."},{"p":"/guide/#reminders-habits","t":"Habits","g":"Reminders","x":"How do I turn it on? In Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Reminders, turn on Habits. Habits is then a place in the Reminders: above your lists in the Mac’s sidebar, and as a row at the head of the iPhone’s lists. Making a habit On the Mac, choose Reminder ▸ New Habit…. On the iPhone, in Habits, tap +. Give it a name and how often: every day, on some days of the week, or so many times a week, from one to six. Turn on Remind Me, and it reminds you at that time on each day it is due and not yet done. Marking it The circle beside a habit marks it done today, and tapping it again takes that back. A habit can be marked on any day. A square in the grid of the last twelve weeks marks that day. What it shows Plain counts: how many of the last 30 days, or how many this week, such as “3 of 4 this week”. From two in a row, its streak, such as “5 days in a row”: in days for a daily habit, in weeks for one with so many times a week, and in due days for one on some days. A day not yet done today doesn’t end a streak; a day missed does. A grid of the past twelve weeks, a column a week. Each square is done, missed (a day it was due and wasn’t done), not due, or still to come. The reminder A habit’s reminder comes on each day it is due and not yet done, for the next eight days. It says the habit’s name, or just “Reminder” with the alert words hidden. It has no Done or Snooze buttons. Today and deleting On the Mac, on Today, the habits due today and not yet done are with the reminders, each with its circle. A deleted habit waits in Recently Deleted with its days."},{"p":"/guide/#reminders-maintenance-logs","t":"Home and car maintenance logs","g":"Reminders","x":"How do I turn it on? In Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Reminders, turn on Maintenance Logs. A group called Logs appears under your lists, one row for each log. On the iPhone it is a row, Logs, at the head of the lists. Logs and entries New Log makes a log for a car, the house or the furnace, read by miles, kilometers, hours or nothing. Log Something adds an entry: what was done, the day, its cost and the reading. A log says what its entries cost this year and in all. Recurring items New Recurring Item… says how often something is due: every so many months, every so many miles, or both, whichever comes first. For example, “Oil change: every 6 months or 5,000 miles”. Log It…, beside an item, logs it. What is due next Stonefold works it out. By time, from the last entry logged for the item, or the day the item was made. By reading, from the last entry with a reading, plus the step. With both, whichever comes first. A due day by the reading is worked out from the log’s readings once two are at least a month apart. A reminder, if you want one Choose a list under Remind Me In, and the item’s next due is a reminder there, such as “Oil change · Car”, moved each time you log it. When a reading counts, its notes say “Or at 45,000 miles”. Because it is an ordinary reminder, it shows on Today, on the calendar with Reminders on the Calendar, and in your alerts, with Done and Snooze. Deleting A deleted log waits in Recently Deleted with its entries and items, and comes back with them."},{"p":"/guide/#reminders-boards","t":"A list as a board","g":"Reminders","x":"How do I turn it on? On the Mac, turn on Boards in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Reminders, choose a list, then Reminder ▸ View as Board ( ⌥⌘B ). On the iPhone, turn on Boards in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Reminders, open a list, and choose View as Board in its menu. Its reminders are cards in columns: To Do, Doing and Done to start. Moving cards On the Mac, drag a card to another column, or choose it and press ⌥⌘[ or ⌥⌘] to move it left or right. The arrow keys move through the cards, and Space marks the one chosen done. On the iPhone, touch and hold a card, then drag it to another column. Tap a card to open its reminder. Done means done: a card moved into Done is marked done, and one moved out of it is not done again. Columns and cards A column’s menu renames it, moves it, or deletes it, and Add Column… adds one before Done. A card shows its day and, when its notes hold lines that begin “- [ ]” or “- [x]”, how many of them are ticked."},{"p":"/guide/#reminders-copying-from-apples-reminders","t":"Copying from Apple’s Reminders","g":"Reminders","x":"How do I do it? Open Settings ▸ Backups and Export and choose Bring Mine Over… for the Reminders. It reads the lists and reminders that Apple’s Reminders holds on this device, and says what would come. Choose Bring In. Nothing is copied until you do. The first time, the system asks whether the app may use your reminders. Answer no, and nothing is read or copied. The app then says where to allow it. What comes over Each list comes over as a new list, after yours, with its titles, notes, days and times, the repeats this app can follow and the early alerts it has. Done reminders stay behind, and so do locations, pictures, tags, flags and priorities. Afterwards It runs once, and nothing stays linked. Nothing in Apple’s Reminders is changed, and a change made there afterwards does not show here."},{"p":"/guide/#reminders-deleted-reminders","t":"Deleted reminders","g":"Reminders","x":"A deleted reminder waits in Recently Deleted until you empty it. Nothing is removed after a number of days. Choose it there to put it back as it was. Wiping Delete Now wipes one thing, and Empty Recently Deleted wipes everything there. Each asks first. What is wiped can’t be brought back. Backups made before then still hold it until they age out."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-writing-a-note","t":"Writing a note","g":"Notes","x":"On the Mac, press ⌘N and type. On the iPhone, tap the pencil and type. There is nothing to save: your words are saved as you type. The title and the list A note’s title is its first line. The list shows pinned notes first, then the one you changed most recently. Plain text A note is plain text. The app keeps its words exactly as they are in the note."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-finding-a-note","t":"Finding a note","g":"Notes","x":"A note is found when each word you type starts a word in it, in any order: “gro lis” finds “Grocery list”. Capitals and accents don’t matter. Notes whose title matches come first. Recently Deleted is not searched."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-folders-checklists-formatting","t":"Folders, checklists and formatting","g":"Notes","x":"Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes turns on more: Folders, Checklists and Simple formatting. Each is on once the notes are first started. On the Mac, Features at the foot of the sidebar shows the same switches. The kinds of note, Tags, Templates and Saved Views have their own switches in the same place. Turning a feature off hides it and changes nothing you wrote. Turn it on again and everything is as it was."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-how-formatting-is-written","t":"How formatting is written","g":"Notes","x":"Checklists and formatting are plain Markdown marks in your note’s own text, so an export stays readable. The marks stay visible, faint, so what you see is exactly what’s saved. # Heading is a heading. ## and ### make smaller ones. **bold** is bold, and *italics* is italics. [words](https://…) is a link. - item is a bulleted list, and 1. item a numbered one. - [ ] item is a checklist item, and - [x] item once it is ticked. Three backticks on a line of their own begin a block of code, with a language’s name after them, and three more end it. See Code notes . Links open only when you ask. There are no previews and no pictures from the web."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-notes-as-files","t":"Your notes as files","g":"Notes","x":"Export Notes… In Settings ▸ Backups and Export, Export Notes… writes one plain text file for each note into a new folder inside the one you choose: .md with Checklists or Simple formatting on, .txt otherwise. Each of your folders becomes a folder there. Recently Deleted is left out. A deck is written as a file of its cards, the kind Anki brings in, without pictures or your answers. The files aren’t encrypted, so anyone who can open that folder can read them. The app asks before it writes them. Bring In Notes… In the same place, Bring In Notes… reads a folder of .md or .txt files. Each file becomes a note, nothing in the folder is changed, and words already here aren’t brought in twice."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-tags-and-templates","t":"Tags and templates","g":"Notes","x":"Tags (Mac) Turn on Tags in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes, then write a word with # in front of it anywhere in a note, such as #garden. The sidebar lists every tag with how many notes hold it. Choose one to see those notes. A # followed by a space starts a heading, not a tag, and a tag needs at least one letter. Capitals and accents don’t matter: #Garden and #garden are one tag. Nothing is added to a note for its tags. They are read from its words. Templates Turn on Templates in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes, and any note that holds #template is a template. The template stays as it is. On the Mac, Notes ▸ New Note from Template… ( ⌥⌘N ) lists them, and so does the New Note button beside search. Choose one to start a new note with its words, without the #template. On the iPhone, touch and hold the pencil to see them, and choose one to start a new note with its words, without the #template."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-saved-views","t":"Saved views in your notes","g":"Notes","x":"How do I save one? In Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes, turn on Saved Views. Show a search, a tag or a folder in the list. Choose Notes ▸ Save This View… ( ⌥⌘S ) and give it a name. Using your views Your views are in the sidebar, under Saved Views. Choose one to show its tag or its folder, and its search, again. Control-click a view to rename it, move it up or down, or delete it, or drag it to another place. A deleted view waits in Recently Deleted, like a note. Export Notes… writes your views beside your notes, in a file named Saved Views.csv."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-kinds-of-note","t":"Kinds of note","g":"Notes","x":"A kind of note is still a note. It is stored in the same encrypted place and opens with the same unlock. You choose the kind when you make the note. How do I turn a kind on? Authenticator, Journal, Recipes, Logins, Binder, Code and Flashcards are on once the notes are first started. Each has a switch in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes, as the password generator does. How do I make one? New Note stays quick. On the Mac, ⌘N and, on the iPhone, a tap on the pencil still make a plain note at once. On the iPhone, touch and hold the pencil to see the list. On the Mac, use the New Note button beside the search field. Its arrow opens the list, which shows once there is more than a plain note to offer. The list holds Plain Note, then each kind whose switch is on, then From Your Templates while Templates is on. The kinds Journal , one entry for each day. Recipes , to cook from. Logins , usernames and passwords. Binder , what your family would need if something happened to you. Code , code in a monospace face, colored by its language. Deck , flashcards to study. Authenticator , sign-in codes for two-step sign-in. A kind is chosen when a note is made and never changed. Search, the recovery sheet and export treat each kind as the page for it says."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-journal","t":"Journal","g":"Notes","x":"How do I turn it on? Journal is on once the notes are first started. If it is off, turn it on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes. Writing today’s entry Choose Journal in New Note’s list, or use the command box. On the Mac, Notes ▸ Today’s Journal Entry ( ⌃⌘J ) opens today’s entry, or starts it. An entry is typed as a plain note is, and saves as you type. One entry for each day The day and an optional mood belong to the entry, not to its words, so typing can never move an entry to another day. An entry’s day never changes once it is stored. The entry’s page has a strip of the last fourteen days. Each day you have written is marked. Choose any day on the strip to open that day’s entry, or to start it. A day after today can’t be chosen. A menu holds five moods: Hard, Low, Okay, Good and Great. Or choose none. The list names each entry by its day, with its first words under it. Export An exported entry has a first line such as journal: 2026-11-21 , with , mood: good if it has one, before its words. Bring In Notes… brings it back as an entry of that day."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-recipes","t":"Recipes","g":"Notes","x":"How do I turn it on? Recipes is on once the notes are first started. If it is off, turn it on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes. On the Mac, choose Notes ▸ New Recipe, then Add Ingredients and Steps…. On the iPhone, touch and hold the pencil and choose Recipe, then tap Add Ingredients and Steps…. Type its servings, times, tags, ingredients and steps, and choose Save. What a recipe holds A recipe has a name, its servings, prep and cook times in minutes, tags, ingredients and numbered steps. It is stored as plain text: the name on the first line, then lines that each start with a word of the format: servings: , prep: , cook: , tag: , ingredient: and step: . Other lines are kept after them. Cooking from it The screen shows the recipe to cook from: its times, its tags, Serves with − and + that scales every ingredient’s amount, the ingredients, and the numbered steps. The scaling is for this look only. It isn’t kept. Edit Recipe… changes it later, in the same form: name, servings, prep and cook minutes, tags, and the ingredients and the steps, one on each line. It is one change, and Undo takes it back. How scaling works An amount at the start of an ingredient, such as 2, 1.5, 1/2, 1 1/2 or ½, is multiplied by the new servings over the old. It is written back as a whole number, a common fraction (halves, thirds, quarters or eighths) or one decimal place. Finding a recipe Search reads the name, the ingredients, the steps and the tags. A recipe’s tags are its own, listed with it. They are not the notes’ # tags."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-logins","t":"Logins","g":"Notes","x":"How do I turn it on? Logins is on once the notes are first started. If it is off, turn it on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes. On the Mac, choose Notes ▸ New Logins Note, or Logins from the New Note button beside search. On the iPhone, touch and hold the pencil and choose Logins. What a login holds Each login has a site, a username, a password, an address and notes. A Logins note shows the note’s name, then each login’s site, username and address, with the password as dots. The note takes no typing: each change is one of the notes’ own actions, with one Undo step. Adding a login Choose Add Login…. Fill in Site, User Name, Password, Address and Notes. Generate Password…, beside Password, opens the password generator in place and puts its password in the field. Save it. The first login waits for the notes’ recovery sheet, as the first Authenticator account does. Edit Login… and Delete Login… are on each login. Delete asks first. On the Mac, you can drag the logins into another order. Seeing and copying a password Show Password shows one password for 30 seconds, then hides it again. Copy Password and Copy User Name copy for this device only, and clear the clipboard after 90 seconds if it is still there. The first time you Show or Copy Password after the app unlocks, it asks you to show the device is yours: Face ID or Touch ID, or the passcode or the Mac’s password. Once passed, passwords and sign-in codes both show until the app locks. A Logins note doesn’t ask when it opens, so you may open it for a username. What it is not There is no autofill, no browser extension and no network. The notes passphrase A Logins note opens only with the notes passphrase, typed once each time the app is unlocked, on the notes’ own screen or on the note. Face ID and Touch ID open the notes, but not these. With no notes passphrase yet, making your first Logins note asks you to set one, and the note is made once it is set. Search and export Search holds the note’s name and each login’s site, username and address, never a password or a login’s notes. Export Notes… and Export Everything leave the passwords and each login’s own notes out unless you choose to put them in, which asks first that this is your device. Export Notes… offers Choose a Folder and Include Passwords…. Export Everything has a switch, off to start. Export as CSV…, on a Logins note, writes its logins in the columns Title, URL, Username, Password and Notes, so you can move them to another app. It warns you first that the file holds every password as plain text. Bring In Notes… brings an exported Logins note back as one."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-password-generator","t":"The password generator","g":"Notes","x":"How do I turn it on? Password Generator is on once the notes are first started. If it is off, turn it on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes. Inside Add Login and Edit Login it is always there, with Logins. Where is it? On the Mac, View ▸ Generate Password… ( ⌃⌘G ) opens it in a small window of its own, and the command box finds it there. On the iPhone, tap Generate Password under More, beside the calculator, or in Settings with one section kept. Three kinds Password is 8 to 64 characters long, from letters, figures and symbols as you choose. With Leave out look-alikes on, which it is to start, characters easily taken for one another (0, O, o, 1, l, I and |) are left out. At least one of each kind you chose is always in it. PIN is 4 to 12 figures. It says it is meant for a lock that stops after a few wrong tries. Groups of Six are three groups of six letters and figures joined by dashes, such as xxxxxx-xxxxxx-xxxxxx, with one capital and one figure among them. How strong it is It shows the strength in bits, worked out exactly from the number of passwords the settings can make, never from the password shown: “About 89 bits: very strong”. The word is weak under 40 bits, fair under 60, strong under 80 and very strong from 80. Copying Copy copies for this device only, and clears the clipboard after 90 seconds if it is still there."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-binder","t":"A binder for your family","g":"Notes","x":"How do I turn it on? Binder is on once the notes are first started. If it is off, turn it on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes. On the Mac, choose Notes ▸ New Binder. On the iPhone, touch and hold the pencil and choose Binder. A new binder is named Important Information, until you change it. What a binder holds Its sections are ready: Emergency contacts, Doctors and medications, Insurance, Bank and retirement accounts, Where the papers are, Who to call, Online accounts and Anything else. Choose Add… in a section to fill it in, and leave the rest empty. Each entry has a Title, Details and, if you want one, a Number. Change… and Delete… are on each entry. Delete asks first, and Undo brings it back. In the list of notes, a binder says how many entries it has, not what is in them. Numbers An account, policy or member number shows as dots until you show this is your device, the same as a password. Show Number, Hide Number and Copy Number are on the entry. Online accounts Online accounts are named by site only. Add Sites from Your Logins adds the sites in your Logins notes by name. No password ever goes in the binder. Making a PDF Make a PDF… makes the binder’s pages on your device, to print or hand to someone you trust. The PDF isn’t encrypted, and it says so before it is made. Numbers show only their last four characters unless you choose Include Numbers…, which asks first that this is your device. The lock By default, anyone who can unlock your device can open the binder. For a lock of its own, turn on Lock the notes with a passphrase in Settings ▸ The Lock."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-code","t":"Code notes","g":"Notes","x":"How do I turn it on? Code is on once the notes are first started. If it is off, turn it on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes. On the Mac, choose Notes ▸ New Code Note. On the iPhone, touch and hold the pencil and choose Code. A new one is named Untitled Code, until you change it. What a Code note is Its words are in a monospace face, colored by the language you choose from the menu above it: Swift, Python, JavaScript and TypeScript, HTML, CSS, JSON, YAML, Markdown, Shell, SQL or Plain Text. On the Mac, they have line numbers too. Nothing in a Code note is ever run. It keeps the code, and that is all. On the Mac, Tab puts in spaces, Shift-Tab takes them out, and Return keeps the line’s indent. On the iPhone, Return keeps the line’s indent. Finding and copying On the Mac, Notes ▸ Find and Replace… ( ⌥⌘F ) finds words in a Code note and changes them. Copy All copies the whole note. Export, and code inside other notes Export Notes… writes a Code note as a file of its language, such as .py or .swift, and Bring In Notes… brings such a file back as a Code note. With Simple formatting on, three backticks on a line of their own begin a block of code in any note, with a language’s name after them, and three more end it. See How formatting is written ."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-flashcards","t":"Flashcards","g":"Notes","x":"How do I turn it on? Flashcards is on once the notes are first started. If it is off, turn it on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes. Then choose Deck in New Note’s list, or on the Mac Notes ▸ New Deck. A deck holds cards, and Study lists every deck with how many cards are new, learning and due. Adding cards Add Card… makes a card of one of four kinds: Basic has a front and a back. Basic and Reversed is studied both ways, as two cards. Type the Answer asks you to type the back before it is shown. Cloze hides parts of a sentence. In Water boils at {{c1::100}} degrees {{c2::Celsius}} , each part between the marks is hidden on a card of its own, one for each number. A hint can follow a second ::, as in {{c1::100::a number}} . Pictures on cards A card can have a picture on its front, its back or both, chosen from your photos or your files, or dragged onto the card on the Mac. A picture is made smaller on the way in and keeps only its pixels: where and when it was taken, and the camera, are left behind. Give it a description, and VoiceOver reads that. Search reads a card’s words, never its pictures. Bringing cards in, and sending them out Bring In Cards… adds the cards in a text file to the deck: a file Anki writes with Export ▸ Notes in Plain Text, a file Quizlet exports with a tab between each term and its definition, or a spreadsheet saved as CSV. Choose the file’s pictures with it, and the cards that name them get them. Export Cards… writes the deck as a file Anki can bring in, with the pictures it names beside it. Anki’s own deck packages aren’t read. Export Notes… and Export Everything write each deck’s cards as the same kind of file, without pictures or your answers. See Leaving with your things . Deleting a deck A deleted deck takes its cards, pictures and answers to Recently Deleted with it. Choose it there to put it back, and they come back too."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-studying-a-deck","t":"Studying a deck","g":"Notes","x":"A round of study Study Now shows each card’s front. Show the answer, then say how it went: Again if you forgot, Hard, Good, or Easy. Each button shows when the card comes back after that answer. A new card comes back in minutes at first; once learned, in days. When a card comes back When a card comes back is worked out with FSRS, the method Anki uses by default, from all your answers to it: the better you know a card, the longer it waits. It aims for you to remember 90 percent of cards when they come back. Deck Settings… can change that, and how many new cards and reviews each day brings. A study day starts at 4 in the morning. Keys On the Mac, Space or Return shows the answer, 1 to 4 answer, ⌘Z takes the last answer back, and Esc ends. On the iPhone, tap the card to show the answer, and Undo takes the last answer back. When you are done When a deck is done for the day it says so, and how many cards tomorrow brings. Stats shows today’s answers, the cards due in the next seven days, and how often you remembered learned cards over the last thirty days. On the Mac, Today and the status line say how many cards there are to study."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-sign-in-codes","t":"Authenticator notes","g":"Notes","x":"How do I set it up? Authenticator is on once the notes are first started. If it is off, turn it on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes. On the Mac, choose Notes ▸ New Authenticator Note ( ⌃⌘N ). On the iPhone, touch and hold the pencil and choose Authenticator. Choose Add Account…, and type or paste the key a site shows when you turn on two-step sign-in with an authenticator app. Add Account… also has More Options: Algorithm (SHA-1, SHA-256 or SHA-512), Digits (6 or 8) and New code every (30, 60 or 15 seconds). Change these only if the site says to. Most use SHA-1, six digits and 30 seconds. Using the codes The note shows each account’s code as it changes, with a ring for the seconds it has left. Click or tap a code to copy it. On the Mac, the app takes the code off the clipboard after a minute, or as it quits, unless you’ve copied something since. Universal Clipboard doesn’t offer it on your other devices. On the iPhone, it stays on this iPhone’s clipboard for a minute, and Universal Clipboard doesn’t offer it on your other devices. An Authenticator note opens only with the notes passphrase, as a Logins note does. Face ID and Touch ID open the notes, but not these. The check before codes show The first time you open an Authenticator note after unlocking the app, it asks for Touch ID or your Mac’s password (on the iPhone, Face ID, Touch ID or your passcode) before it shows the codes. If you cancel, the names still show and the codes stay hidden, with Show Codes to ask again. Looking after your accounts Edit Names… changes the site’s and your own names for an account, never its key or how its codes are made. A new key is a new account. Delete Account… asks first. Before you delete one, turn off two-step sign-in at the site, or be sure you can sign in another way. Undo takes it back. Your accounts are kept in the note, with your notes, encrypted. The app asks you to make a recovery sheet before the first account, and it’s wise to keep each site’s own backup codes too. Search finds an Authenticator note by its name and its accounts’ names, never by a key. Export Notes… writes each account’s key as readable text, and says so before it writes anything. If you turn Authenticator off, it stops being offered when you make a note, and notes already made keep opening and showing their codes."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-the-keyboard-in-notes","t":"The keyboard in your notes","g":"Notes","x":"On the Mac, spelling is corrected and checked as you type in your notes. To change that, Control-click in a note and choose Spelling and Grammar. On the iPhone, spelling is corrected and checked as you type. Settings ▸ Notes: the keyboard changes that. Apple’s Writing Tools and inline predictions are off in your notes and their search. Apple doesn’t say that any app’s settings stop the keyboard learning, so this app doesn’t claim to."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-voiceover-in-notes","t":"VoiceOver in your notes","g":"Notes","x":"With Simple formatting on, VoiceOver’s rotor lists a note’s headings and links and moves from one to the next. On the Mac, press Control-Option-U. On the iPhone, turn two fingers on the screen. The marks themselves (#, **, - [ ]) are read as written. How much of them you hear follows VoiceOver’s punctuation setting. On the iPhone, VoiceOver says “heading” as it reads one. With Checklists on, Tick or Untick is a VoiceOver action on the note. On the iPhone, swipe up or down to it. In an Authenticator note, VoiceOver hears an account’s names, then its code a digit at a time and the seconds left, with Copy Code as the hint."},{"p":"/guide/#notes-deleted-notes","t":"Deleted notes","g":"Notes","x":"A deleted note waits in Recently Deleted until you empty it. Nothing is removed after a number of days. Choose it there to put it back, and you can change it again. A deleted deck goes there with its cards, pictures and answers, and comes back with them. When you empty Recently Deleted, the words are wiped from your notes on this device. Backups made before then, Time Machine’s and iCloud’s among them, keep them until they age out."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-adding-a-purchase","t":"Adding a purchase","g":"Budget","x":"How do I add one? On the Mac, with the Budget showing, press ⌘N . Type the amount, then the payee: choose one you’ve used before from the list under the field, and it fills in the category it had last time. Check the category, then click Add. On the iPhone, tap Add at the bottom of a budget screen. Type the amount, tap Next, then type the payee: tap one you’ve used before in the list under it, and it fills in the category it had last time. Check the category, then tap Add. Money in, transfers and more Money out is the usual kind. Choose money in or a transfer at the top. Yesterday is one tap on the sheet itself. The account, another date and a memo are under More. Once it’s saved, the sheet says what that category has left, with Undo beside it."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-bills-and-paydays","t":"Bills and paydays ask first","g":"Budget","x":"Nothing is recorded by itself. On its due day a bill shows under Waiting for You on Home, with Paid, Different Amount… and Not Yet. A payday asks the same way, with It Came. One tap records the transaction with the bill’s amount, account and category. Typing the payment yourself counts just the same. On the calendar and in Reminders With the Calendar or the Reminders kept too, the “Bills on the Calendar and in Reminders” switch, in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Budget, shows a bill on its due day in the calendar, and with today’s reminders while it waits. Among the reminders, Paid, Different Amount… and Not Yet work as they do on Home."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-where-you-stand","t":"Where you stand, and safe to spend","g":"Budget","x":"The gauge Home’s gauge says whether the month is in good shape, tight or short: one track, red at the left for short, yellow in the middle for tight and green at the right for good shape, with a pointer above it where the month stands, and “Short” and “Good shape” under its ends. The sentence under it says the same in words. It comes from three answers: what’s safe to spend, whether this month’s bills are covered, and your pace through the month. The further left the pointer, the worse the month. Each third of the track is one of the three words, and inside its third the pointer follows the same answers. When short, it’s further left the further below zero the money would go, against what you have now and the pay still to come this month. When tight, it’s further left when nothing is safe to spend, when bills would take more of the money set aside, or the further behind your pace is. In good shape, the plan’s day itself is the middle of its third, and spending slower moves it right. Hiding the gauge On the Mac, turn off Show the Gauge in Settings, under Home, or Control-click Where You Stand and choose Hide Gauge. It’s hidden on this Mac only, and the sentence stays. On the iPhone, turn off Show the Gauge in Settings, under Home. It’s hidden on this iPhone only, and the sentence stays. Safe to spend Safe to spend is what your everyday categories have left this month, lowered when the plan asks for more money than there is. It never goes below zero. Can I Afford It? Can I Afford It? answers for one purchase. When the answer is yes but the purchase would still make the month tight or short, a second line says so. How it was worked out Under the gauge, click (or on the iPhone, tap) How This Was Worked Out for every answer’s sums. It’s arithmetic on what you typed, nothing more. Pace shows once there’s an everyday plan to keep pace with."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-checking-a-balance","t":"Checking a balance with your bank","g":"Budget","x":"Open an account and choose Check Balance…. It shows what your budget says the account holds. If your bank shows the same, choose It Matches, and the account is marked as checked today. If not, choose It Doesn’t and type the bank’s figure. You’ll see the difference, and can mark recent transactions as not at the bank yet. Still off? Add what was missed, fix a typo, or add a correction: one entry, clearly labelled."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-the-plan","t":"The plan, and changing it","g":"Budget","x":"On the Mac Plan is a grid: each category down the side, with what’s spent and what’s left this month, then what’s planned for each of the next twelve months, and the twelve added up. Each group’s line adds up its categories, and the line along the foot adds up them all. Type a figure in a month to change it: it becomes that category’s amount for that month of the year, every year, as Set by Month… sets it, and this month’s also changes this month’s plan. A figure the plan would give anyway is left to the plan. With Envelope budgeting on, amounts are set on the Budget screen instead, and the months are read only. Right-click a category or a group to rename, hide or move it. On the iPhone Plan lists each category with its amount this month, what’s spent and what’s left. Change an amount for this month only or from now on, or set different amounts for different months of the year. With Envelope budgeting on, amounts are set on the Budget screen instead. Categories and groups can be added, renamed, hidden and moved. A budget is meant to change from month to month. Changing it isn’t falling behind."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-working-in-the-grid","t":"Working in the grid","g":"Budget","x":"An account’s register and Plan are grids. Click a cell, or move with the arrow keys. Tab goes along a row and on to the next. Type to replace what’s in the chosen cell, or press Return or click it again to change it as it is. While typing, Return keeps it and goes down, Tab keeps it and goes right, and Esc leaves the cell as it was. Undo, several rows and copying down Each change is one step of Edit ▸ Undo. Shift with the up or down arrow, or a click with Shift, chooses more rows, and the totals row along the foot then adds up just those. ⌘D copies the first chosen row’s cell down to the others. In a register Click a column’s heading to sort by it, again for the other way, and a third time for the register’s own order. A split’s lines, the other account of a transfer, and a row’s account are changed in the entry strip above the grid: typing in one of those cells opens it. In the cleared column, Space marks the chosen rows cleared or not. New transactions are added with ⌘N , as before."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-copying-and-pasting-rows","t":"Copying and pasting rows","g":"Budget","x":"Copying In the grid, ⌘C copies the chosen rows the way a spreadsheet app takes them. They’re marked for this Mac only, so Universal Clipboard doesn’t offer them on your other devices. While they’re on the clipboard, any app on this Mac can read them, as with anything you copy. Pasting into a register ⌘V in a register reads rows copied from a spreadsheet as new transactions, in the register’s own order of columns from Date to Inflow, or in any order when the first row names the columns. A column named Amount is one figure, below zero for money out. In All Accounts an Account column comes after Date. Before anything is added, a sheet shows each row as it was read, and why any row can’t be kept. Choose Keep, and each row is added as a change of its own. Edit ▸ Undo takes the whole paste back. A paste never changes a transaction already there. Pasting into the plan ⌘V in Plan puts figures in the months from the chosen cell, rightward and downward, shown first in the same way. Only the months change."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-more-for-the-budget","t":"More for the budget","g":"Budget","x":"Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Budget turns on more: Envelope budgeting (the Budget screen and Unassigned), Income planning, Reports, and Advanced transactions (Reconcile and a budget year). Net worth, Debt payoff and Retirement planning are switches there too. Each is on once your budget is made. With Envelope budgeting on, what your budget’s setup plans for its first month is what is assigned. On the Mac, Features at the foot of the sidebar shows the same switches. Turning a feature off hides its screens again. What you entered stays. Bills on the calendar and in Reminders has its own switch, in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Budget. See Bills and paydays ask first ."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-net-worth","t":"Net worth","g":"Budget","x":"How do I turn it on? Net worth is on once your budget is made, and Net Worth has a place of its own: in the Mac’s sidebar, and under More on the iPhone. If it is off, turn it on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Budget. What it shows What is owned and what is owed today, each account with its balance, the totals, and a chart over one, two or three years. Accounts outside the budget are marked so. It is typed by hand, so Stonefold never connects to a bank. Keeping values up to date Update Values… lists each account outside the budget, with what it holds now and a field for its value now (for a loan, what is owed). Save writes one entry for each one changed, labelled “Value update” and dated today, in one Undo step. Add an Asset… opens the new account sheet with Type already set to “Asset or Investment (tracking only)”, and Include in budget off. A value is a correction, as a balance check’s is, so the history and the chart follow it."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-debt-payoff","t":"Debt payoff","g":"Budget","x":"How do I turn it on? Debt payoff is on once your budget is made, and Debt Payoff has a place of its own. If it is off, turn it on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Budget. What you enter Each card and loan account that you owe on is listed with its balance, and has fields for its rate (APR) and its minimum. You can also type in debts by name and balance. Then set the extra you can pay each month, and the order. The orders Smallest balance first. Highest rate first. Your own order. Each month interest is added and every minimum is paid. What is left of the minimums and the extra goes to the first debt in the order still owed, then the next. A debt paid off frees its minimum for the rest. The order is fixed from the starting balances and rates. What it shows When each debt is paid off, the interest in all, the smallest-balance and highest-rate orders side by side, and the plan month by month, starting next month. If a plan would take more than 100 years, it says “Not in 100 years”. What you type in Debt Payoff is forgotten when the app locks, unless you choose Save Plan, which keeps it in your budget. When Debt Payoff opens with nothing typed, the saved plan comes back. Exporting the plan Export Plan saves the plan as a spreadsheet or a CSV file, after saying the file isn’t encrypted. Saving the plan Save Plan keeps one plan in your own budget’s encrypted file: each account’s APR and minimum, the debts you typed in, the extra and the order. It is not offered in the sample budget. A line says where the plan stands: “Not saved”, “Saved in your budget” or “Changed since it was saved”. Go Back to the Saved Plan brings the saved one back, and Delete the Saved Plan… takes it out of your budget; what is on screen stays until the app locks."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-retirement-planner","t":"The retirement planner","g":"Budget","x":"How do I turn it on? Retirement planning is on once your budget is made. If it is off, turn it on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Budget. The Mac’s budget sidebar has Retirement, after Reports, and the command box finds it and New Retirement Plan…. Each one is a “retirement plan”: “Plan” is already the budget’s monthly plan. Plans are kept in the budget’s file, encrypted. On the iPhone, Retirement is in the budget’s list of screens. Starting a plan New Retirement Plan… asks four things: the year you were born, the age you’d like to retire at, what you spend a year, and what you earn a year. It fills in the rest. It counts your budget’s own accounts, other than cards and loans, as cash, and an account you track outside the budget only if you choose it, as taxable: count investments, not a house or a car. You set each one’s type. To start it assumes an end age of 95, inflation 3%, tax 15%, returns of 6% and 2%, volatility 12% and a safe withdrawal rate of 4%. The plan’s page says what it assumed, each with Change beside it. On the Mac, click any part of the plan to change it, or + beside a list to add to it. On the iPhone, tap New Retirement Plan… to make one, tap any part of it to change it, or + beside a list to add to it. The page The answer comes first: whether your money lasts to 95 or runs out at an age, the chance of success, and the target and when it is met. Then a chart of net worth over your life, with retirement and milestones marked, and the plan’s parts in short lists: Accounts, Income, Spending, Saving, Milestones and Social Security. Each part is a sheet. On the Mac, By Year shows every year: age, income, Social Security, taxes, spending, saved, taken out and net worth, in today’s money or that year’s. On the iPhone, By Year shows every year’s net worth in today’s money. Change… opens More About This Plan: the ages, the rates, the withdrawal order, the leftover account, the safe rate and the number of tries. Comparing plans To compare, choose Duplicate from the plan’s menu, then change the copy: retiring at 60 and at 65, say. On the Mac, Compare draws up to three plans side by side. What goes in a plan Accounts: cash, taxable, tax-deferred (such as a 401(k) or traditional IRA), Roth and HSA. Each balance is read from one of your budget’s accounts, or typed, and grows at the plan’s rate for its type, or a rate of its own. Streams: income, spending, and contributions into an account, by you or by an employer. Each is in today’s money and rises with prices unless you say not, from now, an age, retirement or a milestone, up to another. Milestones: retiring, and any others at an age: a house, college, a windfall. Each can bring money once, and money yearly for some years. Social Security: typed from your own statement: the monthly benefit at full retirement age (67 for anyone born in 1960 or later, or set yours) and the age you will claim it, 62 to 70. The claiming age changes the benefit by the Social Security Act’s rule for early and late claims. It rises with prices. Taxes: one rate you type, on taxed income and Social Security less what is put in before tax, on all of a tax-deferred withdrawal, and on the growth share of a taxable one. Withdrawals: when a year spends more than comes in, from the accounts in a plain order: cash, taxable, tax-deferred, Roth, HSA. You can change the order under More. The target and the chance of success The target is a year of retirement’s spending over a safe withdrawal rate, in today’s money, and the age at which your accounts first hold it. The chance of success is worked out from a thousand lives on your device, each drawing every year’s market return from a normal curve with the expected return as its middle and a volatility you set as its spread. Cash never moves with the markets. The same plan always gives the same chance. It is shown beside the plain projection, with the band one life in ten either side. What it leaves out It is an estimate, not financial advice. Today is taken as the start of this year. A year grows first, and its money comes and goes at its end. Growth isn’t taxed until it is taken out. No tax tables, early-withdrawal penalties, contribution limits or required withdrawals are counted, and the plan follows one person’s ages: a partner’s income is a stream from an age. Like everything Stonefold works out, it is plain arithmetic, with no AI."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-the-sample-budget","t":"The sample budget","g":"Budget","x":"Look Around a Sample Budget, offered in the Budget section before you choose Set Up Your Budget, opens a made-up household with three years of spending, bills and pay. Try anything in it. Nothing in it is kept: it goes when you leave it, and it’s made fresh next time. Start My Own Budget takes you to a budget of your own."},{"p":"/guide/#budget-budgeting-words","t":"Budgeting words","g":"Budget","x":"Each word has one short meaning and an everyday example. Words that belong to a feature only show when that feature is on. Activity What went in or out of a category this month; spending shows as a minus. For example: Two grocery trips of 80 and 45 make Groceries’ activity −125. Shown with Envelope budgeting on. Assigned What you give a category this month, to spend or to keep. For example: Assigning 600 to Groceries in October plans 600 of food for October. Shown with Envelope budgeting on. Available What a category has left now: what it carried in, plus what was assigned, plus its activity. For example: 600 assigned and 125 spent leave 475 available. Shown with Envelope budgeting on. Budget Accounts Accounts you budget from, such as checking, savings and cards. For example: Checking, savings and your cards: the money your budget and Safe to Spend count. Carried In What a category had left at the end of last month, which it keeps this month. For example: 40 left in Dining Out on September 30 starts October with 40 carried in. Shown with Envelope budgeting on. Cleared Transactions the bank has already posted. For example: A purchase on your October statement is cleared. Due A bill not paid yet this month, with its due day still to come. For example: A streaming service is due October 28. Until you enter its payment, it reads Due. Features Extra tools you can turn on in Settings, such as envelope budgeting, income planning and reports. For example: Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Budget: turn on Reports, and Reports appears in the sidebar. Funded A bill or target already has all the money it needs this month. For example: Internet costs 89.99 and its category holds 89.99: funded before it’s due. Shown with Envelope budgeting on. Overdue Its due day passed and no payment has been entered. For example: Water was due October 5, and by October 23 no payment has been entered. Overspent A category spent more than it had, so its Available is below zero. For example: Dining Out had 50 and spent 70: it’s 20 overspent until you cover it from another category. Shown with Envelope budgeting on. Paid This month’s payment for the bill is in your transactions. For example: Electric’s 188.60 payment on October 15 marks October paid. Partial Some of what a bill needs this month is set aside, but not all of it. For example: 50 set aside for an 89.99 bill is partial: it needs 39.99 more. Shown with Envelope budgeting on. Projected End of Month The money with no job left at the month’s end, once pay still expected arrives and the budget is spent. For example: 20,000 in cash, 500 of bills to pay, 1,000 still budgeted and 2,000 of pay to come: 20,500. Shown with Income planning on. Reconcile Checking your budget’s balance against a statement’s, then locking the transactions you checked. For example: The statement says 1,000 on October 9 and so does your budget: one click marks those rows checked. Shown with Advanced transactions on. Safe to Spend What’s left this month for everyday things and fun, after bills and money set aside for later. For example: Everyday has 420 left and Just for Fun 120: 540 is safe to spend, about 108 a day for 5 days. Starting Balance What an account held on its first day in your budget. For example: A card that owed 412 on July 1 starts at −412. Target What a category aims to have, every month or by a date, so you know how much to assign. For example: A 600-a-month Groceries target asks for 600 each month; Assign to All Targets gives it. Shown with Envelope budgeting on. Tracking Accounts Accounts you watch without budgeting from them, such as a mortgage or investments. For example: A 401(k) shows its balance and history, but isn’t divided into categories. Transfer Money moved between two of your accounts: not income, and not spending. For example: Paying the card from checking is a transfer: both balances change, and no category does. True Expenses Costs that come now and then, saved for a little each month so they don’t catch you out. For example: Car repairs, gifts, a yearly subscription: 36 a month for the car is there at its next service. Shown with Envelope budgeting on. Unassigned Money you have that isn’t planned for anything yet: give it a job by assigning it to categories. For example: You were paid 3,000 and gave 2,500 to categories: 500 is Unassigned. Shown with Envelope budgeting on. Uncleared Transactions you’ve entered that the bank hasn’t posted yet. For example: A payment you entered today, before the bank has posted it. Unfunded Nothing is set aside for a bill yet this month. For example: Set Aside on the Dashboard assigns what the bills due soon still need. Shown with Envelope budgeting on. Working Balance Cleared plus uncleared: what the account holds once everything posts. For example: 1,200 cleared + −200 uncleared = 1,000 working balance."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-tools","t":"What Tools is","g":"Tools","x":"What is in Tools? On the Mac, Tools keeps the timers, alarms, stopwatch, world clock, countdowns and focus timer under Time, and the calculator under Figures, with Mortgage, Car Loan, Refinance, Savings Goal, Percent while Money Tools is on in Settings ▸ Sections, as it is to start, Converters while it is on there, and Password Generator while it is on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes. Tools ▸ Calculator ( ⌥⌘7 ) opens the calculator. On the iPhone, Tools opens on a tile for each tool: the timers, alarms, stopwatch, world clock, countdowns and focus timer under Time, and the calculator, its money tools and converters, and Password Generator while it is on, under Figures. Tap a tile to open it, and Back to see them all again. The calculator here is the same one that opens from anywhere: it keeps nothing, and forgets every sum at the lock. How do I turn it on? Welcome keeps Tools, as it keeps every part, and Settings ▸ Sections turns it off and on. Timers, Stopwatch, World Clock, Countdowns, Focus Timer and Converters are each on once Tools are first started, and each is turned off and on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Tools, and Converters in Settings ▸ Sections."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-stopwatch","t":"The stopwatch","g":"Tools","x":"How do I use it? On the Mac, in Stopwatch, choose Start, or press ⌘Return from anywhere in Tools. Lap ( ⌘L ) marks a lap while it runs. Stop holds it, Go On carries on, and the last button ( ⌘R ) clears it once it is stopped. On the iPhone, in Stopwatch, tap Start. Lap marks a lap while it runs. Stop holds it, Go On carries on, and the last button clears it once it is stopped. The laps The laps are listed newest first, each with its own length. The quickest and the slowest are named once there are two. While the app is closed The stopwatch keeps the moment it started, not a count, so it keeps time while the app is closed or locked."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-timers","t":"Timers","g":"Tools","x":"How do I set one? On the Mac, choose New Timer… ( ⌘N ), set the hours, minutes and seconds or choose one of the lengths under them, give it a name if you like, and press Return. On the iPhone, tap New Timer, set the hours, minutes and seconds or choose one of the lengths under them, give it a name if you like, and tap Start Timer. A timer can be from 1 second to 99 hours 59 minutes 59 seconds. The ready-made lengths are 1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 30 and 60 minutes. Running timers Several can run at once. Each shows what is left and the time it rings. Pause holds it, Go On carries on, and Cancel puts it back to its whole length. A timer stays in the list to start again until you delete it. A running timer survives the app closing: it keeps the moment it ends."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-alarms","t":"Alarms","g":"Tools","x":"How do I set one? On the Mac, choose New Alarm… ( ⇧⌘N ), pick the time, the days it repeats on and a label, and choose Save. On the iPhone, tap New Alarm, pick the time, the days it repeats on and a label, and tap Save. With no days chosen it rings once, at the next time it comes, and then reads as off. An alarm’s switch turns it off and on. Click or tap it to change it. What it says When an alarm or a timer rings it says only “Alarm” or “Timer”, never its label or name, because what it says shows on the Lock Screen. How it rings On the Mac, an alarm rings as a notification with a sound, while notifications for the app are allowed in System Settings ▸ Notifications. The system keeps 64 alerts for one app, and the soonest of the alarms, timers, reminders, events and bills are the ones set. On the iPhone, the app asks to set alarms when you set your first. Allowed, alarms and timers ring through silent mode and a Focus, as an alarm clock does. Not allowed, they ring as notifications instead."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-world-clock","t":"The world clock","g":"Tools","x":"On the Mac, choose Add City… ( ⌥⌘N ), type part of a city’s name, and choose it. On the iPhone, tap Add City, type part of a city’s name, and choose it. Each city shows its time, whether it is today, yesterday or tomorrow there, and how far ahead or behind it is. The cities and their clock changes come from this device’s own list of time zones. Nothing is looked up anywhere."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-countdowns","t":"Countdowns","g":"Tools","x":"How do I turn it on? In Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Tools, turn on Countdowns. Countdowns is then a place in Tools. Making one On the Mac, choose New Countdown… ( ⌥⇧⌘N ). On the iPhone, tap New Countdown. Tap one to change it. Give it a name and a day, and a time if it has one. Every Year counts to the same day next year once it comes, as a birthday does. A countdown to 29 February comes on 28 February in other years. What it says Each says the days until it: “in 12 days”. Within two days it says the hours as well, and on the day itself with no time it says “Today”. One that has passed moves to Passed and says how long ago it was. Nothing is deleted by itself. On the Mac, the countdowns to come are also on Today, and the soonest is on the status line."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-focus-timer","t":"The focus timer","g":"Tools","x":"How do I turn it on? In Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Tools, turn on Focus Timer. Focus is then a place in Tools, after Timers. How does it run? Choose Start Focus (on the Mac, ⌥⌘Return ). A focus runs, then a short break, again and again, with a long break after the last focus of the set. Then it rests until you start it again. Pause holds it, and Go On carries on. Skip Break goes straight to the next focus, or, from the long break, ends the set. Stop Focus puts it away. It starts at 25 minutes of focus, 5 of break and a long break of 15 after every fourth. Lengths… changes them while no focus is going. A focus can be 1 to 120 minutes, a break 1 to 60, and a long break can come after every 2 to 8 focuses. What it rings and counts It rings at the end of each focus and each break, as a timer rings, saying only “Focus done” or “Break done”. Each focus that ran its whole length counts toward today’s sessions. It shows that count in plain words. It keeps time while the app is closed."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-calculator","t":"The calculator","g":"Tools","x":"Where is it? On the Mac, View ▸ Calculator ( ⌃⌘C ) opens a small window of its own. The command box finds it too. Locked, it shows only “Locked”. On the iPhone, it is a sheet from More ▸ Calculator, or from Settings when you keep one section. It has a pad of keys. How do I use it? Type a sum: figures, +, - or −, * × or x, / or ÷, brackets and %. Return works it out and leaves the answer to go on from. Esc clears it, and Esc again closes it. In the calculator’s other modes, Esc closes the calculator. ↑ and ↓ bring back the sums before. Copy Answer ( ⇧⌘C on the Mac) copies the answer. How it works things out It is decimal, not binary, so 0.1 + 0.2 is 0.3. × and ÷ come before + and −, from the left, with brackets first. A % is per hundred, and after + or − it is a share of what comes before: 80 + 15% is 92. It uses the device’s decimal mark and its thousands mark between figures. When a sum can’t be worked out it says why: “That isn’t a sum”, “Nothing can be divided by zero.” or “The answer is too large to show.” What it keeps What it worked out is kept in memory only, fifty sums at most, and forgotten when the app locks. Copy Answer touches the clipboard only when you choose it, and marks what it copies for this device only. Mortgage, Car Loan, Refinance and Savings Goal can keep a scenario in your budget, if you choose. Beside the sums, in the menu at the top of the calculator, are Mortgage, Car Loan, Refinance, Savings Goal, Percent and Converters. See The mortgage calculator and the pages after it."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-mortgage-calculator","t":"The mortgage calculator","g":"Tools","x":"Where is it? Choose Mortgage in the menu at the top of the calculator, which holds Sums, Mortgage, Car Loan, Refinance, Savings Goal, Percent and Converters. On the Mac they are in the View menu too, and while you keep Tools they are under Figures there, on the Mac’s sidebar and the iPhone’s tiles. They are there from the start: Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Money Tools hides them, and shows them again. Converters, there too, is on once Tools are first started. Every rate is one you type: nothing is looked up, and no rates come with the app. What you type is forgotten when the app locks, unless you save it as a scenario. What you enter The price, and the down payment as an amount or a percent. The interest rate in percent a year, the term in years, and the month of the first payment. It is next month unless you choose another. 15 and 30 years are shown side by side. Property tax a year, home insurance a year, PMI in percent a year, and HOA dues a month, if you have them. Extra payments: a monthly amount, a yearly amount, which is paid with every twelfth payment, and one amount once, at a payment number. Typed fields are read as sums, so “250,000” and “250000 × 0.2” both work, with or without a currency sign, and a rate’s “6.5%” is 6.5 percent. Each extra goes to what you owe. What it shows The monthly payment, broken down into principal and interest, tax, insurance, PMI and HOA. The payoff month, the interest in all, and what the extra payments save in interest and in months. PMI is paid until you owe 80% of the price, and not at all with 20% down. It shows how long PMI is paid and what it comes to. The schedule The schedule shows by year, with each year’s months under it, or by payment. Export Schedule saves it as a spreadsheet or a CSV file, after saying the file isn’t encrypted. On the Mac it is saved where you choose, and on the iPhone it is handed to the iPhone’s own sheet for sending or saving. Saving a scenario While your own budget is open, Mortgage has a part called Saved in Your Budget. Save Scenario… asks for a name, and keeps the figures you typed, as you typed them, in your budget’s encrypted file. An empty name becomes “Mortgage 1”. Choose a saved scenario to bring its figures back, and use the trash button beside it to delete it. It is not offered in the sample budget. A scenario saved by a later version of the app can’t be read by this one, and it says so."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-car-loan-calculator","t":"The car loan calculator","g":"Tools","x":"Choose Car Loan in the menu at the top of the calculator. Every rate is one you type: nothing is looked up, and what you type is forgotten when the app locks, unless you save it as a scenario. What you enter The car: the price, the down payment, the trade-in’s value and what is owed on it. Sales tax in percent, and fees. The APR, in percent a year. Sales tax is on the price less the trade-in, and never below nothing. Fees, and anything owed beyond the trade-in’s value, are borrowed with the car. What it shows Terms Compared shows 36, 48, 60 and 72 months side by side: the payment, the interest, the total of payments, and the total cost, which is the price, the tax, the fees and the interest. Saving a scenario While your own budget is open, Car Loan has a part called Saved in Your Budget. Save Scenario… asks for a name, and keeps the figures you typed, as you typed them, in your budget’s encrypted file. An empty name becomes “Car Loan 1”. Choose a saved scenario to bring its figures back, and use the trash button beside it to delete it. It is not offered in the sample budget. A scenario saved by a later version of the app can’t be read by this one, and it says so."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-refinance-calculator","t":"The refinance calculator","g":"Tools","x":"Choose Refinance in the menu at the top of the calculator. Every rate is one you type: nothing is looked up, and what you type is forgotten when the app locks, unless you save it as a scenario. What you enter The Loan Now: what you owe, the interest rate now in percent a year, and the years left. Part of a year counts, so 27.5 is 330 payments. The New Loan: the new interest rate in percent a year, the new term in years, and the closing costs. Add the Costs to the Loan, if you will borrow them. Costs not added to the loan are paid at the start. The payment now is worked out from what you owe, the rate and the years left, so it may differ from yours by a little. What it shows The payment now, the new payment, and how much less or more it is each month. Break-Even: the months until what you save each month pays back the costs, rounded up to a whole month, and the month that falls in. It says Never when the new payment isn’t lower, and At once when there are no costs. The interest left on the loan now against the interest on the new one, and what each costs in all from here, with the costs paid at the start counted with the new loan. When a lower payment costs more in all, it says so in a sentence, and names the longer term when that is why. Saving a scenario While your own budget is open, Refinance has a part called Saved in Your Budget. Save Scenario… asks for a name, and keeps the figures you typed, as you typed them, in your budget’s encrypted file. Choose a saved scenario to bring its figures back, and use the trash button beside it to delete it. It is not offered in the sample budget."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-savings-goal-calculator","t":"The savings goal calculator","g":"Tools","x":"Choose Savings Goal in the menu at the top of the calculator. The growth rate is one you type: nothing is looked up, and what you type is forgotten when the app locks, unless you save it as a scenario. Two ways to work it out What It Grows To takes what is saved now, what you put in each month and a growth rate in percent a year, over some years or, with Until a Date on, until a date. It shows what there is at the end. What to Put In takes a goal, what is saved now and a growth rate, over some years or until a date. It shows the least you can put in each month, in whole cents, to reach the goal by then. When what is saved now reaches it with nothing more put in, it says so. How it is worked out Growth is added each month on what is there, at the rate you type, and rounded to the cent. Each month’s deposit goes in at its end, from next month. What it shows What there is at the end, what you put in and what it grew in all, and Year by Year: each year’s deposits, its growth and what there is at its end. Saving a scenario While your own budget is open, Savings Goal has a part called Saved in Your Budget. Save Scenario… asks for a name, and keeps the figures you typed, as you typed them, in your budget’s encrypted file. Choose a saved scenario to bring its figures back, and use the trash button beside it to delete it. It is not offered in the sample budget."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-percent-calculator","t":"The percent calculator","g":"Tools","x":"Choose Percent in the menu at the top of the calculator. What you type is forgotten when the app locks, and there is nothing to save. A Percent of a Figure: a percent of a figure, such as 15% of 80. One Figure as a Percent of Another: what percent one figure is of the whole. Change From One Figure to Another: the change, up or down, as a percent of the first figure. A Price After a Discount: the price, the percent off and sales tax, which is on the price after the discount. Leave the tax empty for none. It works in decimal, so the answers are exact, and it shows a percent to two places at most. The discount and the tax are rounded to the cent."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-converters","t":"Converters","g":"Tools","x":"Where are they? Choose Converters in the menu at the top of the calculator. Converters has a second choice: Units, Dates, Tip or Currency. Nothing is fetched, and what you type is forgotten when the app locks. Units Choose a measure: Length, Weight, Volume, Temperature, Area, Speed or Cooking. Type a value, choose From and To, and the answer shows. Swap turns them round. Units are named as the system names them. Dates Days Between counts the days between two dates. A Date and Some Days gives the date a number of days away, with the weekday. Type a minus for days before. Tip Type the bill, the tip percent and the number of people. It shows the tip, the total, and what each pays, rounded up to the cent. When the shares come to a little more than the total, it says by how much. Currency Type an amount and a rate, and the answer shows both ways. The rate is the one you type: no rates come with the app, and none are looked up."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-the-clock-beside-your-work","t":"Tools beside your work","g":"Tools","x":"View ▸ Panels offers Timers, Stopwatch and World Clock while Tools is kept, and Calculator whatever you keep. Each is a panel round the section on screen, which docks, stacks and collapses as the other panels do. See Panels, and your own layout . A timer started or a lap marked in a panel is Tools’ own. New Timer… and Add City… at a panel’s foot open Tools on that sheet."},{"p":"/guide/#clock-deleting-in-the-clock","t":"Deleting in Tools","g":"Tools","x":"A deleted alarm, timer, city or countdown goes to the Recently Deleted of Tools. Choose it there to put it back. An alarm put back is off until you turn it on. Delete Now… and Empty Recently Deleted… clear it for good. Nothing else is touched. Backups and export The alarms, timers, cities and countdowns of Tools aren’t in Export Everything, and the sheet says so. They are kept with the calendar and reminders, so Save a Copy… of the calendar and reminders carries them."},{"p":"/guide/#files-what-files-is","t":"What Files is","g":"Files","x":"Files keeps the documents you’d rather not lose: IDs, insurance, receipts, warranties and manuals. It is a place inside the app, not a cloud drive. How do I turn it on? Welcome keeps Files, as it keeps every part, and Settings ▸ Sections turns it off and on. Once Files is kept, the app’s lock is on, as it is for the notes. How is it kept? Files keeps a file of its own, encrypted, opened by the same unlock as the notes and the budget. Each document is sealed by itself, with a key of its own, inside that encrypted storage on this device. Nothing in Files is copied into your other files, a setting, an alert or a log. The recovery sheet covers Files too, with a code of its own. Backups of Files hold the list of your documents and what opens them, not a second copy of each document."},{"p":"/guide/#files-bringing-files-in","t":"Bringing files in","g":"Files","x":"On the Mac Drag files from the Finder onto the list, or onto a folder in the sidebar. Or choose Files ▸ Add Files… ( ⌘N ). A folder dragged in comes in as a folder, with what is in it. On the iPhone Tap Add, then Add Files to choose files. Or tap Add, then Scan Document to scan pages with the camera. A scan is kept as one PDF. The first time you scan, the system asks whether the app may use the camera. The camera is used for nothing else. From another app, choose this app among the places a file can be sent, and the file comes into Files. The system copies it into the app’s own folder, where it waits, unreadable while the iPhone is locked and left out of its backups, until it is sealed into Files and that copy is deleted. What can come in Any kind of file. Each file is sealed by itself, with a key of its own, inside the app’s encrypted storage on this device. A file may be up to 2 GB."},{"p":"/guide/#files-folders-names-and-details","t":"Folders, names and details","g":"Files","x":"Folders and names On the Mac, New Folder… ( ⇧⌘N ) makes a folder inside the one on screen. Right-click a file or a folder to rename it, move it or delete it. Rename… is ⌘R . On the iPhone, Add ▸ New Folder makes a folder inside the one on screen. Swipe a row to delete it. A file’s own screen has Move To and Rename…. A folder can’t go into itself or into a folder inside it. Deleting a folder sends what is in it to Recently Deleted with it. Details A file’s details say what kind it is: ID, Insurance, Receipt, Warranty, Manual or Other. If you set them, they also say the day it was purchased and the day it expires. A file within 30 days of expiring says “Expires soon”."},{"p":"/guide/#files-finding-a-file","t":"Finding a file","g":"Files","x":"Search Search finds a file by its name, and by the words inside it: a PDF’s own text, a text file’s words, and, with Find Words in Pictures and Scans on, the words in pictures and scans. Every word you type must be in the name or in the words found. Capitals and accents don’t matter. Words in pictures Words in pictures are read on this device by Apple’s Vision framework, with no language model. Nothing is sent anywhere. The switch is in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Files, and is on once Files is first started. While it is on, it looks through the files it hasn’t looked at yet, and says so under the switch. Sorting On the Mac, click a heading of the list to sort by it. Click it again to turn the order round. You can sort by name, date added, kind, expiry date or size. On the iPhone, Add ▸ Sort By changes the order of the list. Folders come first."},{"p":"/guide/#files-looking-at-a-file","t":"Looking at a file","g":"Files","x":"A PDF, a picture or a text file is shown in its details, drawn from memory: no copy is written anywhere to show it. The app’s own preview opens a file of up to 200 MB. A larger one offers Save a Copy… instead. Another kind of file shows its name, kind and size, and can be saved out with Save a Copy… and opened in another app. Save a Copy… Save a Copy… writes the file as it is into a folder you choose. It is then outside the app, and no longer sealed."},{"p":"/guide/#files-expiry-reminders","t":"Reminders before a file expires","g":"Files","x":"How do I turn it on? Remind Me Before a File Expires, in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Files, is on once Files is first started. The first time a document has an Expires day, the system asks whether the app may send alerts. An alert then comes at 9:00 a week, 30 days or 60 days before a file’s Expires day. You choose which. It starts at 30 days. What the alert says The alert says only “A document expires soon.” It never names the file, because what an alert says shows on the Lock Screen. Nothing about a file goes into an alert or into your other files."},{"p":"/guide/#files-deleting-in-files","t":"Deleting in Files","g":"Files","x":"A deleted file or folder goes to Files’ Recently Deleted. Choose it there to put it back. A folder comes back with what was deleted with it. Wiping Delete Now… and Empty Recently Deleted… clear it for good: its details and its key are wiped and its sealed contents deleted. Backups made before then still hold its listing and the words found in it until they age out, never its contents. Taking files with you Save a Copy… writes one file out as it is. Export Everything copies every file not in Recently Deleted out as it is, in its folders, into a folder called Files. Nothing in that folder is encrypted. See Leaving with your things ."},{"p":"/guide/#mac-today-and-the-status-line","t":"Today, and the status line","g":"On the Mac","x":"Today With two or more sections kept, Today is in the middle of Home, the first tab, which View ▸ Today ( ⌃⌘T ) goes to as well. It shows the day across what you keep: today’s and tomorrow’s events, the reminders that are late and due today, the notes you changed last, and the budget’s Safe to Spend with the bills that wait and those coming up. Double-click a row, or choose it and press Return, to open it in its own section. Click a reminder’s circle, or choose it and press Space, to mark it done. Edit ▸ Undo takes that back. Each part lists eight rows and says how many more there are. Settings ▸ Look ▸ Today chooses which parts Today shows, which column each is in, and their order. A part you leave out can still be a panel. Standard Today puts it back as it was. Today only reads what you keep: looking at it changes nothing and keeps nothing. A budget or notes locked with their own passphrase say only that they show when unlocked. If Home was in front when you last used the app, it opens on Home. The status line The status line along the foot of the window counts the day: events today and the next one still to start, reminders due and late, notes, the cards to study with Flashcards on, and bills waiting. Click one to go to its section. View ▸ Hide Status Line puts it away, and this Mac remembers. The pane at the right The pane at the right of a section stays as you leave it: the calendar’s day, with the chosen event’s place and notes under the list; the reminders’ inspector; the budget’s inspector. ⌥⌘I shows or hides it, and this Mac remembers."},{"p":"/guide/#mac-tabs-and-desks","t":"Tabs and desks","g":"On the Mac","x":"The tabs Home and each section you keep are tabs that stay, first in the bar. Home holds Today, and each section’s tab holds that section, with whatever panels you put beside it. Your desks follow, each a layout for one job with its own panels and its own middle, so the same panel can be on as many tabs as you like. Showing a section, by its tab, its ⌘ number or the command box, goes to its own tab and never changes a desk. The tabs run along the top of the window. Settings ▸ Look ▸ Tabs ▸ Side puts them down the left side instead. Your first desks You start with two desks made of the sections you keep: Money, with the Budget, Where You Stand and Bills This Month, and Plans, with the Calendar in the middle, the Reminders on the right and the Notes along the bottom. Making a desk + at the end of the tabs, File ▸ New Desk or ⌘T opens the New Desk page: a Blank Desk, or a ready-made one, each drawn as a small picture of where its panels go: Money, Plans, Bills and Paydays, Month Review, Study, Writing, Projects, A Big Purchase, Paperwork and Daily Plan, as many as the sections you keep can fill. Nothing is made until you pick one, and Esc or ⌘W puts the page away. Under them are the desks you closed lately, to reopen. A ready-made desk is named for what it is until you rename it, and Put Back as It Started puts it back as it was made. Add Panel shows a panel on the tab in front, or puts one away, with the same choices as View ▸ Panels. A desk with nothing on it says so, with Add Panel and a way back to the ready-made desks, and a whole section you add to it goes in its middle. View ▸ Panels ▸ New Desk From starts one from a layout round what is in the middle: One Section at a Time, Today Beside a Section, or Two Sections Side by Side. Closing, moving and renaming Click a desk’s × or press ⌘W to close it. Nothing in it is deleted: File ▸ Reopen Closed Desk ( ⇧⌘T ) brings back the last one you closed as it was, and the last six can come back. ⌘W never closes a tab that stays, and ⇧⌘W closes the window, which locks the app. Drag a desk’s tab to move it, double-click its name to rename it, or Control-click it for Rename, Duplicate, Put Back as It Started, Move Left and Move Right, Close Desk, Close Other Desks and Reopen Closed Desk. ⌃Tab goes to the next tab and ⌃⇧Tab to the one before. ⌃⌥ and a number, 1 to 5, goes to the desk in that place. Put Back as It Started, in a tab’s menu or View ▸ Panels, puts Home, Money and Plans back with their own panels, and any other tab with none. What a desk keeps This Mac keeps the tabs, and the app opens on the one you left. Each desk keeps where you were in it: the note open in the Notes, the day chosen in the Calendar and the month your budget shows. A desk holds only where each panel is and what is in its middle, never what a panel shows. A desk’s own name, up to 40 characters, is kept in your everyday file, encrypted with your calendar and reminders."},{"p":"/guide/#mac-panels-and-layouts","t":"Panels, and your own layout","g":"On the Mac","x":"Showing a panel View ▸ Panels shows a panel beside the section on screen: Events, today’s and tomorrow’s; Reminders, those late and due today; Notes, the ones you changed last; Money, the budget’s Safe to Spend and its bills. Each is Today’s part by itself, there whatever section you are in, for each section you keep. Your own lists: View ▸ Panels ▸ Lists shows one of your reminders lists, a notes folder with Folders on, or a saved view of either as a panel. Pictures: View ▸ Panels ▸ Pictures ▸ Add a Picture… puts a picture you choose in a panel of its own. It is kept inside your everyday file, encrypted with your calendar and reminders. Charts and figures: the budget’s Home in a panel: where you stand, the month ahead, and pace through the month. Sections: View ▸ Panels ▸ Sections puts a whole section beside the one on screen: the Notes beside the Calendar, say. It shows the section as you last left it in its own sidebar, and you can work in it as you would in the middle. While its list or one of its fields has the keyboard, its own menus and ⌘ keys act on it, as they would in the middle. To go to it in its own tab, click the arrows in its title bar, Show in Its Tab, or press its ⌘ number. Where a section is the middle, its panel waits. Edit ▸ Undo takes back the last change made in the window, wherever it was made. Moving and docking Drag a panel’s name to move it, and the place it would go lights up. Near the left, the right or the foot of the section on screen, it docks there. Over another panel’s name, it joins it as a tab. Over the rest of one, it goes beside it. Let go anywhere else and nothing moves. Control-click a title bar to dock it on the left, on the right or along the bottom. Collapsing and resizing The pin in a title bar collapses the panel to a strip at its edge, where its name runs along the window. Click the name to slide the panel out over the section; click the section, or press Esc, to put it back. The × closes a panel, and View ▸ Panels shows it again. Drag the line between a panel and the section to make it wider or narrower. Keys Hide Panels ( ⌃⌘P ) puts them all away and brings them back. Next Panel ( ⌃⌘] ) and Previous Panel ( ⌃⌘[ ) move the keyboard from the section to each panel in turn. In a panel, ↑ and ↓ choose a row, Return or a double-click opens it in its own section, and Space marks a reminder done. Desks Each desk keeps its own panels for one job, and this Mac keeps them. See Tabs and desks ."},{"p":"/guide/#mac-the-keyboard","t":"Using the keyboard","g":"On the Mac","x":"⌘/ lists every key, for every section you keep, in Help ▸ Keyboard Shortcuts. Hold ⌘ for a moment and the keys for what is on screen show until you let go. ⌘K opens the command box. G then a letter goes somewhere, wherever you aren’t typing. ⌘1 and on switch between the sections you keep, in the order shown. ⌘N is “new” in the section on screen. The command box Type a few letters of a command, a place, a note, a list or a setting, choose with ↑ and ↓, and press Return. Esc closes it. The box keeps no history of what you type in it. Type a line with a day or a time, such as “dentist fri 3pm”, and it offers to add it as an event or a reminder on that day at that time. It reads the same days and times as Typed Dates in Reminders: today, tomorrow, a weekday, a date like “nov 20”, and times like 3pm or 15:30. A time with no am or pm, like “at 3”, isn’t read. Any line can also become a new note. Type a line with an amount, such as “coffee 4.50”, and the box offers New Transaction: the budget’s Add sheet opens with the payee and the amount filled in, and nothing is saved until you press Add. With a kind of note turned on in Settings ▸ Sections ▸ Notes, it offers that kind too, named by the line: a recipe, a logins note, a binder, an Authenticator note, a Code note or a deck of flashcards, or the line added to today’s journal entry. Once you type, the box also finds your events from the past month to a year ahead, your reminders not done and those done in the past month, and your budget’s transactions from about the past year, by payee or memo. Choose one to go to it: an event on its day, a reminder in its list, a transaction in its account’s register. Two-key jumps G, then a letter, goes somewhere wherever you aren’t typing: T Today, with two or more sections kept, C the Calendar, R the Reminders, N the Notes, B the Budget, K Tools, F Files, S Settings, H Help. Only the sections you keep have a letter, and after the G a hint shows them. Tools is K, since T is Today’s. Moving in lists Shown by a key, a section’s list has the keyboard: ↑ and ↓ move through it, Return opens the chosen note or reminder, and Esc goes from a note’s words back to the list. Tab moves between the sidebar and the list. ⌥⌘1 and up are the places inside a section, where it has them. Every key For all of them, by section, see Every keyboard shortcut . Keys of your own To give a command a key of your own, turn on Change Keys in Settings, under Look. Each section’s commands are listed with their keys: choose Change… and press the new keys, with ⌘ or ⌃. A key the Mac or the whole app already uses, or that another command of the same section has, is refused, and the app says why. Standard puts a key back, and turning Change Keys off puts every key back. Your keys wait until it is on again."},{"p":"/guide/#mac-every-key","t":"Every keyboard shortcut","g":"On the Mac","x":"These are the keys the Mac app starts with, as Help ▸ Keyboard Shortcuts ( ⌘/ ) lists them: first the app’s own, then each section’s. Hold ⌘ for a moment and the app shows the keys for what is on screen. A key that needs a feature says which one. Only the sections you keep have keys. A key works in its section while that section is on screen, so ⌘N is “new” in whichever one you are in. With Change Keys on in Settings, under Look, you can give any command in a section a key of your own. See Using the keyboard . The app Calendar Reminders Notes Budget Tools Files The app ⌘K Open the command box: find a command, a place, a note or a list, or add something ⌃⌘L Lock the app ⌘? Open Help ⌘/ Show these shortcuts ⌘, Open Settings ⌃⌘, Show Settings in the window ⌃⌘C Open the calculator ⌃⌘G Make a password, with Password Generator on In the command box ↑ ↓ Choose a row (⌃N and ⌃P too) ↩ Do it, or add the line esc Close the box Going places G and the letter after it work wherever you aren’t typing, and only for the sections you keep. ⌘1…⌘6 Go to a section, in the order shown. The last number is the number of sections you keep. ⌃⌘T Show Today, across the sections. It is there once you keep two or more. G T Go to Today (two or more sections kept) G C Go to the Calendar G R Go to the Reminders G N Go to the Notes G B Go to the Budget G K Go to Tools G F Go to Files G S Go to Settings G H Go to Help Hold ⌘ Show the keys for what is on screen Moving around ⇥ ⇧⇥ Between the sidebar, the list and what is open ↑ ↓ The next or the last row of a list ↩ Open the chosen note or reminder to change it esc From a note’s words back to the list Text size For the whole window, while the Calendar or the Notes, which have their own, aren’t on screen. ⌘+ Larger Text ⌘- Smaller Text ⌘0 Standard Text Size Panels The first three once a panel is shown from View ▸ Panels. The desk keys work from anywhere in the window. ⌃⌘P Hide the panels, or show them again ⌃⌘] Move the keyboard to the next panel, and back to the section ⌃⌘[ Move the keyboard to the panel before ⌃⌥1…⌃⌥5 Go to the desk in that place among the desks Calendar ⌘N New Event ⌘T Today ⌘[ Previous Month ⌘] Next Month ⌥⌘1 Month ⌥⌘2 Recently Deleted ⌥⌘I Show Day / Hide Day ⌘+ Bigger Text ⌘- Smaller Text ⌘0 Actual Size Week With Week on, in Settings. ⌥⌘3 Week Year With Year on, in Settings. ⌥⌘4 Year ⌥⌘[ Previous Year ⌥⌘] Next Year Templates With Templates on, in Settings. ⌥⌘N New Event from Template… In the month and the year, the arrow keys move by a day and by a week. In the day’s list, Return changes the selected event. Reminders ⌘N New Reminder ⇧⌘N New List ⇧⌘T Today ⇧⌘C Mark Done / Mark Not Done ⌘⌫ Delete ⌥⌘I Show Inspector / Hide Inspector Saved Views With Saved Views on, in Settings. ⌥⌘S Save This View… Templates With Templates on, in Settings. ⌥⌘N New List from Template… Boards With Boards on, in Settings. ⌥⌘B View as Board / View as List ⌥⌘[ Move Card Left ⌥⌘] Move Card Right Notes ⌘N New Note ⌘F Search Notes ⇧⌘P Pin Note / Unpin Note ⌘⌫ Delete Note ⌘R Put a deleted note back ⌥⌘⌫ Delete Now… ⇧⌘⌫ Empty Recently Deleted… ⇧⌘E Export Notes… ⇧⌘I Bring In Notes… ⌥⌘1 All Notes ⌥⌘2 Recently Deleted ⌘+ Bigger Text ⌘- Smaller Text ⌘0 Actual Size Folders With Folders on, in Settings. ⇧⌘N New Folder ⇧⌘M Move To… Templates With Templates on, in Settings. ⌥⌘N New Note from Template… Authenticator With Authenticator on, in Settings. ⌃⌘N New Authenticator Note Journal With Journal on, in Settings. ⌃⌘J Today’s Journal Entry Code With Code on, in Settings. ⌥⌘F Find and Replace… Saved Views With Saved Views on, in Settings. ⌥⌘S Save This View… Checklists With Checklists on, in Settings. ⇧⌘L Checklist ⇧⌘U Tick or Untick Simple formatting With Simple formatting on, in Settings. ⌃⌘1 Heading ⌃⌘2 Subheading ⌃⌘3 Small Heading ⌃⌘0 Body ⌘B Bold ⌘I Italic ⇧⌘K Add Link… ⇧⌘7 Bulleted List ⇧⌘8 Numbered List Budget Anywhere in the Budget ⌥⌘N New account ⌘Z ⇧⌘Z Undo or redo a change to transactions or assigned amounts ↩ In a sheet: the highlighted button, such as Save esc In a sheet: Cancel or close it Moving around ⌘F Find transactions ⌥⌘1… Go to the budget’s places in the sidebar, top to bottom ↩ Open or edit the selected row Months On Budget, Income, Budget Comparison and Bills Calendar. ⌘[ Previous month ⌘] Next month ⌘T This month Budget ⇧⌘T Go to a month ⇧⌘M Move money ⌥⌘↑ ⌥⌘↓ Move the selected row up or down ⌥⌘I Show or hide the inspector 0–9 + − Type an amount to assign: 250 sets it, +50 adds, −50 takes away ↩ ⇥ Save it and go down ⇧↩ ⇧⇥ Save it and go up esc Stop editing without saving ← → Fold or unfold a group Transactions ⌘N Add a transaction ⇧⌘C Mark cleared or uncleared ⌘R Reconcile the account ⌫ Delete the selected rows, after asking In the grid In an account’s register and in Plan. ↑ ↓ ← → Move to the next cell ⇥ ⇧⇥ Along a row, and on to the next one ⌘↑ ⌘↓ The first or last row ⌘← ⌘→ A row’s first or last cell ⇧↑ ⇧↓ Choose more rows; the totals row adds them up A–Z 0–9 Type over the chosen cell ↩ Change the chosen cell as it is; while typing, keep it and go down ⇥ While typing: keep it and go right esc While typing: leave the cell as it was Space In the cleared column: mark the chosen rows cleared or not ⌘A Choose every row ⌘C ⌘V Copy the chosen rows; paste rows, shown before they’re kept ⌘D Fill down: the first chosen row’s cell to the others Entering a transaction ⇥ Next field: Date, Payee, Category, Memo, Outflow, Inflow ↩ Save, and start the next one when adding esc Cancel 14 In Date: the 14th of this month. Left empty, it’s today. Dashboard bills F Fund the selected bill P Enter a payment for the selected bill Income S Skip the selected payday, or bring it back E Edit the selected payday’s schedule Reports On the report showing. ⌘P Print the report Single keys like F and P work once you click a row in the table, or press Tab until the table is highlighted. To reach every button with Tab, turn on Keyboard navigation in System Settings ▸ Keyboard. Tools ⌘N New Timer… ⇧⌘N New Alarm… ⌥⌘N Add City… ⌘↩ Start / Stop, the stopwatch ⌘L Lap ⌘R Clear the stopwatch, once it is stopped ⌥⌘1 Timers ⌥⌘2 Alarms ⌥⌘3 Stopwatch ⌥⌘4 World Clock ⌥⌘7 Calculator Countdowns With Countdowns on, in Settings. ⌥⇧⌘N New Countdown… ⌥⌘5 Countdowns Focus Timer With Focus Timer on, in Settings. ⌥⌘↩ Start Focus / Pause Focus ⌥⌘B Skip Break ⌥⌘. Stop Focus ⌥⌘6 Focus Files ⌘N Add Files… ⇧⌘N New Folder… ⌘R Rename… ⇧⌘S Save a Copy… ⌥⌘1 All Files ⌥⌘2 Recently Deleted"},{"p":"/guide/#mac-everything-in-the-window","t":"Everything in the window","g":"On the Mac","x":"What the app’s own menus and Settings hold is in the window too, and the menus and Settings ( ⌘, ) are where they always were. Settings in the window The gear at the foot of the rail, or at the foot of the sidebar when the rail is off, shows Settings in the window, where a section would be: the same groups and switches as the Settings window, in the same order. View ▸ Settings in the Window ( ⌃⌘, ) shows it too. Choose a section or Today to go back, or the button at its head that says where you were. Features Features, at the foot of a section’s sidebar, shows that section’s own switches. Turned on, each adds what it does where it belongs. Turned off, it hides it and touches nothing you keep. With the Budget kept beside the Calendar or the Reminders, the switch that shows bills on the calendar and in Reminders is there too. All Settings shows the rest. The toolbar The magnifying glass opens the command box ( ⌘K ). View holds the View menu: Today, the status line, the panels and their layouts, the text size and the calculator. Menus holds everything in the menus of the section on screen, as the menu bar has them at that moment, keys and all, then what File, Edit, Window and Help hold beyond the Mac’s own items, and last the section’s Features. Three things that had only a menu item have a button of their own: the Reminders’ Inspector, New Note beside the notes’ search field, and the Budget’s Add Transaction on every screen but All Accounts and an account’s, where + adds a row to the list itself."}];
